WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.530
- Hi, good evening everyone.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.000
Welcome to the first event

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.460
of the spring semester
for the Franke Program.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.510
I'm Priya Natarajan.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.630
I happen to be the current
Director of the Franke Program

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.030
in Science and the Humanities,
which as you all know,

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.603
is Yale's flagship
interdisciplinary program.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.280
We have a very exciting roster
of events for this semester.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.650
And today we have a very special event,

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.470
one that charts a personal
intellectual journey

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.950
across disciplines by Dr. Andrew Koh.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:38.040
But first, I would like
to thank our benefactors,

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.740
Mr. and Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.060
whose generosity enables both activities

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.830
at the Franke Program, today's event,

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:50.193
as well as many other
interdisciplinary activities at Yale.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.990
And before I introduce Dr. Koh,

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.880
I'm required to give you this notice

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.280
that Yale University is photographing

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.930
and videotaping this event.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.850
There's a possibility
that you may be included

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:06.150
in these visuals by being
present during these activities.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.730
You consent to the use of
your appearance, likeness,

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:11.910
and voice by Yale for standard nonprofit,

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.920
educational news related promotional

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.560
and or editorial purposes.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.860
And before I forget, as per usual,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.140
after today's talk and the Q an A,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.320
we will be gathering for a
dinner reception upstairs

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.143
in the student lounge
immediately after this event.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:30.300
So on to today's event,

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.380
I am delighted that we
have today as our speaker,

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.350
Dr. Andrew Koh, who is an archeologist

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:40.020
and who is an ancient historian

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.860
with a strong interest in
pharmacology and medicine,

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.860
with a focus on ancient
innovation and technology,

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:54.740
and other discoveries and new knowledge

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.630
that was generated in antiquity

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.740
that we are able to study
today with modern techniques.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.920
He is the museum scientist
for the Yale Peabody Museum,

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.960
and he's a faculty
member in the Department

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.570
of Near Eastern Languages
and Civilizations

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:12.360
and the Council on Archeological Studies.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.540
He's a active field
archeologist who's the PI

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.200
of the Southern Focused
Regional Project in Greece,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.350
and Yale's Ancient Pharmacology Program

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:23.880
at the Peabody Museum.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.800
Before I turn over the floor to him,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.590
I just wanted to mention a
couple of upcoming events.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:32.070
Tomorrow, January 25th,
although it's not our event,

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.410
but it may be of interest to many of you,

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:38.250
is a panel discussion
called Beyond Human Meaning

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:41.550
a conversation with Professor
Gary Tomlinson on the subject

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.250
of his new book of the same title.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.190
And on Tuesday, March
26th, we have a screening

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:50.310
of "Richland" a new
documentary on a small town

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.320
that's struggling with
its identity as a center

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.050
of weapons grade plutonium production.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:58.080
And on April 4th we have a
talk by Christian Cooper,

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.230
the victim of the Central
Park bird watching incident,

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.180
who's an avid bird watcher,

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.310
and he'll be talking to us about that.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:07.680
And we have a talk by
Professor Meredith Broussard

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.910
on big data and the race,
gender, and ability bias

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:14.220
that is embedded in big data.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.460
And then we have a talk
by Professor Anjan Bhullar

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.560
on the main scientific consultants.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.810
He was one of the main
scientific consultants

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.490
for the documentary series that many

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:26.250
of you might have seen,
"Life on Our Planet."

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.860
Anyway, back to today's event.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:32.700
I am really delighted
to invite Dr. Andrew Koh

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:34.260
to come and talk to us.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.940
Thank you so much, Andrew.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.183
- Thank you.
(audience clapping)

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:46.470
Welcome everybody on
this sort of a rainy day.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.210
Thanks to Director, Priya,

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:51.000
and I think Ty is somewhere in the back.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.393
And the Franke family,
as I mentioned to them,

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:56.760
it makes me exceedingly happy

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.430
that this Franke Program exists,

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.470
because that's gonna be
kind of like the theme

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.530
of my time with you today
is kind of the journey

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.800
I've taken, a spoiler alert.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:12.090
As somebody in a kind
sense that said to me,

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:16.500
I'm likely the squarest peg in academia

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:17.670
with a lot of round holes.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.250
So I wanna kinda share that journey

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:25.250
and talk about what experiences I've had.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.540
And also perhaps it might be helpful

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.760
for all the different and
interesting people here.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.900
So I wanna start our
somewhat strange journey,

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:34.733
if I can call it that.

00:04:34.733 --> 00:04:36.210
That was the alternate title, you know,

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.370
the Yale Ancient Pharmacology Program

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:39.750
Exploring Strange New Worlds.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.610
And you'll see here in
a second, it's relevant.

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:43.470
It's my first time flying this.

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:47.100
I'll preface it by saying,
I did not expect this ever.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.320
It was not on my bingo
card, or bucket list,

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.080
or anything like that, to
distribute some of my friends

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.110
who are in fact, die hard Trekkies.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:59.190
What happened was, let's
say late '21, early '22,

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:00.870
very much in the midst of the pandemic,

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.333
I did an interview with a journalist.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.230
You do these things, you don't
know exactly what it's for,

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:09.600
which sounds kind of naive in retrospect.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.840
He was in front of a
fireplace holed up in a cabin,

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:14.400
and he said he was a writer.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.320
And he said he does a
whole bunch of things,

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.620
and this could be relevant
to various things.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.290
So I had very little idea
of what this was all about.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:25.500
What I did know is he
asked me very particular

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:30.500
and astute questions, "What
model of instruments do you use

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.360
when you do your extractions?

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.090
What do you think the
past as the present is?"

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.370
So I did this interview
for almost two hours,

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.830
which was the longest I've
ever done one of these.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:42.690
And I thought maybe it'll
appear in the magazine

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:44.370
as he intimated.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:46.050
And I didn't hear back from him

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:49.950
until I was just back from
my field work in Greece,

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:51.840
let's say July.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.820
He texts me and I later
found out it was him,

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.530
because I'd been with him on the phone,

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:56.460
but I never received the text.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.830
So who knows what this text is all about?

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:02.280
Because all it said was,
"Check out the latest episode

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.860
of Star Trek."
(audience laughing)

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:05.760
Was all it said.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.580
And in fact, I thought it
was some kind of spam email

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:13.230
from some kind of streaming
unit or something like that.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:16.680
Later on, and again, this sounds strange,

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:20.880
somehow in August or September,
something jogged my memory

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:23.850
and said, "Wait, was that the
person who interviewed me?

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:25.830
Like, you know, many months ago?"

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:28.740
I checked and indeed
it was the individual.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:30.960
So then I looked at the
timestamp and I said,

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.490
I didn't even have a subscription
to the streaming service.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.977
So finally I said, you
know, "I'll try that."

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:42.090
And I looked, and to my surprise,

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:47.090
not only did they include some
kind of interpretation of it,

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:50.310
but in the opening segment,
as you'll see here,

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:52.020
it basically sums up my research,

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:55.740
at least as Dave interpreted it.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:58.380
So after I present my, you know,

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:01.440
directly what I've done the
last, let's say 20 years,

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.320
I'll leave it up to you
to judge how good of a job

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.600
they did in terms of interpreting my work.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:09.540
Again, this was not on my
any kind of bucket list.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:11.700
And to my surprise, this happened.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.530
Just to preface it, this is the prequel

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:15.180
to the original series.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.900
And in the original series,
they threw out an occupation

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.090
or discipline of a researcher.

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:23.433
And it never plays a role significantly.

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:27.030
And this discipline that
they invented in the sixties,

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:30.780
in the original series, they
dubbed archeological medicine.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:33.187
And apparently they then, in
more recent memories, said,

00:07:33.187 --> 00:07:34.830
"Oh shoot, we we're doing this prequel

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:36.750
that leads up to the original series.

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:39.690
We actually got a nuance
what this actually means."

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:41.820
And now I think that's
why they went to me.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.567
They somehow found me and said,

00:07:43.567 --> 00:07:46.020
"You're the closest
thing to this, you know,

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:48.810
fictional future discipline."

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:50.640
And to my surprise, again,

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:53.430
this is apparently now "Star
Trek" canon to the chagrin

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:55.800
of my friends, who, when I showed it

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:59.407
to one of my Trekkie friends
up the hill, he was like,

00:07:59.407 --> 00:08:01.740
"You don't even have a paid
subscription to Paramount.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:03.420
This is offensive to me, right?"

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:05.670
So, I have a paid subscription now.

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:08.433
So, this is the clip for two minutes.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:12.903
Of course it has my, here we go,

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:20.313
- [Christine] The log, startdate 1789.3.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.280
We're on route to the Vulcan system.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:26.240
Our mission to survey the moon of Kerkhov

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:28.470
on the far side of the sector.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:33.000
An ancient civilization once
lived there, then vanished.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.680
Long range scans suggest the presence

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.503
of a strange energy anomaly
just above the surface.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.320
A few of us are hoping that
it will provide answers

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:41.970
about where the Kerkhovians went.

00:08:43.574 --> 00:08:45.630
As for the rest of the crew,

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:48.750
the captain has decided to
travel at sub-impulse speeds,

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:51.363
so we can all take in the view.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.410
Now we find ourselves with an excess

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.870
of the most valuable
commodity in the galaxy.

00:08:57.870 --> 00:08:59.643
- I own one of those
- Downtime.

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:03.720
I'm using it to print for an interview

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:05.370
for a two month fellowship

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:07.370
sponsored by the Vulcan Science Academy.

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:10.080
- What are Korby's three principles

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:11.850
of archeological medicine?

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:14.550
- One, medicine is always ancient and new.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:16.530
As cultures' understanding
of what medicine is

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.750
changes over time.
(fists thumping)

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:21.210
Two, the keys to solving our
toughest medical problems

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:23.370
are often found by looking backward.

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:24.203
- Correct.

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:28.500
- Three, as a result,
archeological medicine

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:31.170
is as much a study of
history as it is of science.

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:32.003
- Drink.

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:35.850
- All right, so that's
a, believe it or not,

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:39.630
it's literally the points I
gave them for the most part.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:42.720
So, again, something very unexpected.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:46.230
So now I'll actually
share with you my research

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:48.660
that actually references,

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.753
they distilled into
that two minute segment.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:57.480
Like I said, by many measures, I can say,

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:00.300
happily, I really shouldn't exist

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.040
for reasons I'll detail in a second.

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:04.740
I happily am doing what I'm doing.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:06.300
But there were many moments, you know,

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.760
when I was sitting there in
the nineties at Abbott Labs,

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:10.620
I didn't ever imagine,
I'll be sitting here

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:13.080
in front of you talking about archeology.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:14.550
So that's the journey I've been on,

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:17.520
and it is amusing that
somehow the Star Trek universe

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:19.743
apparently wants me to exist.

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:24.813
You know, more than perhaps one
would automatically presume.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:29.580
I wanna start by conveying
the simple premise,

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:33.060
simply enough, organics were
central to ancient societies,

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:34.173
but are now invisible.

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:37.170
That's something we often forget

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:39.720
when we think about archeology.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.060
We have remnants that
are displayed proudly

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:44.460
on the cover of Archeology Magazine.

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.170
Gold trinkets, vessels,

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:50.580
beautiful funerary vases from,
you know, southern Italy.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:52.710
What we don't know is for the ancients,

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:55.140
it was what was inside of them, typically,

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:55.973
these kind of vessels.

00:10:55.973 --> 00:10:57.900
That was the most important thing.

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:01.290
So we are in fact missing
for the most part,

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.960
the entire reason for the
existence of these vessels.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.310
We're getting the shells, right?

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:08.730
The echoes of what really
mattered often to these ancients.

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:10.230
And for the longest time we thought

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:12.960
that we couldn't really
access this information.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:15.030
There was a general sense
that this was important,

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:16.680
but we can't get to it.

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:20.400
And perhaps as an
ambitious graduate student,

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:23.490
maybe in retrospect, it
was a exercise in hubris.

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:25.830
But nevertheless, I
thought, you know what?

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:27.180
I want to give this a shot.

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:29.970
I have a background.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:33.750
My undergraduate degree was in biophysics

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:36.960
and also Classic and Ancient
Near Eastern studies.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:39.780
So I went to bring that
together, which is not easy,

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:42.630
because I can graduate
school in Philadelphia.

00:11:42.630 --> 00:11:46.620
I had these two streams, but
to kind of bring them together

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:48.060
as this audience would understand,

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:49.740
it wasn't the easiest thing to do.

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:51.630
So I wanna share some of
those experiences now.

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:55.920
So how did we approach, how
did I approach this conundrum

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:58.740
of having really no organic surviving?

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:03.360
So the approach as it's defined
today is archaeochemistry.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:07.170
So what it is, is simply
just the discovery

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:09.840
of ancient organics and
determining their relevance today.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:12.000
How are they different,
how are they similar?

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:13.890
So on and so forth, I'm
always connecting it

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:15.930
to the present and future, of course.

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:18.300
And also utilizing a
transdisciplinary method

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.550
to characterize these
ancient organic residues,

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:23.820
because these things are
elusive, they're ethereal, right?

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:26.580
And as you might know, as someone said,

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:28.590
the chemistry doesn't lie,

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:33.000
but we get these remnants of residues,

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.220
compounds, organic compounds,

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:37.980
and they can come from
different sources, right?

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:42.210
The interpretation is
where we're fallible.

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:43.980
And how we get to that, as you'll see,

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:45.930
is what I'm calling this
transdisciplinary method

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:50.520
where ancient texts, ancient iconography,

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:54.000
all these different other
clues kind of help us hone

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.070
what the chemistry is telling us.

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:57.990
And that's why it's important to do it

00:12:57.990 --> 00:13:00.640
in a very interdisciplinary manner

00:13:02.304 --> 00:13:05.520
as fits what exactly this Franke Program

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:06.603
is trying to support.

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:11.610
And finally, why, again,
why do we do these things?

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:13.830
We want to recover compelling evidence

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:16.350
of what we're calling
non-linear innovation,

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:17.700
technology, craft, et cetera.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.760
Because we know about how
things kind of pass down to us.

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:22.200
You know, we can talk about Roman roads

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:24.300
and they're not that
different from modern roads,

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:27.420
but what particularly intrigues
me is are there instances

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:30.360
where they might have
stumbled upon something.

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:33.270
Something that, you know,
I say right off the bat,

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:36.120
I'm not trying to find the
cure for cancer, for instance.

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:39.450
I don't have those kind of, you know,

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:42.000
aspirations necessarily,
but what really intrigued me

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:45.600
is one of the compounds I found

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:48.960
was heavy doses of zometa acid,

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:50.760
some aldehyde and other things.

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:52.890
And when I did a search
for them, I found out

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:57.890
that they in fact identified
the plant that I was targeting.

00:13:58.230 --> 00:13:59.820
A plant that only grows in today's, like,

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:02.340
let's say modern southwest Turkey

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:04.387
and all the articles were medical, saying,

00:14:04.387 --> 00:14:06.150
"We're using these plants

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:09.150
to try to research medical
cancer treatments."

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:10.560
So I thought that was fascinating.

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:12.150
Like, what does this come to?

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:13.680
How does this come to bear

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:16.410
in terms of how we do
this kind of research?

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:20.910
So I don't think it's a forced,
it's difficult to present

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:24.390
to you that modern pharmacopea
is actually still alive

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:26.700
and kicking today in many ways, right?

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:28.533
Things aren't all from a test tube.

00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:33.600
I was alerted to this because
this might be outdated now.

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:35.400
I'm not sure if Moroccan is still popular,

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:37.710
but my significant other uses this

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:41.100
and what I caught is that, like,

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:44.550
for instance, one of these
smaller vessels to the left,

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:46.040
it was like $80, right?

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:47.640
So I was like, "Oh my gosh, this is $80."

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:50.070
So I looked more into it
because the background

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:51.360
was very fascinating to me.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:54.600
And it's very interesting that to show you

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:55.433
how international it is,

00:14:55.433 --> 00:14:58.440
and it must have been
similar in antiquity.

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:02.460
It's a company, if I remember correctly,

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:05.550
it's a US company based in London

00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:10.550
who sources the botanical
ingredients in Morocco,

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:15.150
but manufactures it in the
Southern Levant of Israel.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:18.363
So again, showing you
what is involved, right?

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:21.303
A very complex situation.

00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:24.510
There's still abundant value today,

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:26.790
but what can we retrieve
from antiquity, right?

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:28.980
That was the million dollar question.

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:32.790
So it started almost
exactly 20 years ago now,

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:35.910
believe it or not, that's when
I started my dissertation,

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:38.467
and I'm calling it the Beginnings At A

00:15:38.467 --> 00:15:40.350
"Minoan Perfumed Oil Workshop."

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:42.450
It's a very technical term
amongst this very small group

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:44.310
of scholars that work on Crete.

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:46.890
If you guys know the
legends of King Minos,

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:47.723
so on and so forth.

00:15:47.723 --> 00:15:50.400
These are the archeologists who specialize

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:55.400
in this very particular time
period of Bronze age Greece,

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:56.910
even though Sir Arthur Evans,

00:15:56.910 --> 00:15:58.950
and many of you have probably
been to Crete and Knossos,

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:02.130
that's that culture and complex society.

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:07.050
So I worked on an American
site in East Crete that summer.

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:11.280
And I wasn't trying to do
archaeochemistry, in fact,

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:13.284
at the moment I was trying
to do everything but,

00:16:13.284 --> 00:16:15.030
I was trying to be as best as possible,

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:17.160
a round peg and not a square peg.

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:20.280
And as we're digging
this site, we came upon

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:22.230
what was immediately labeled the vat room

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:25.570
because they found this vat
that you can see pointed

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:29.370
in C2, right, so, which was very neat.

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:33.360
And as we're excavating,
I noticed several things,

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:34.680
and this is where kind of like

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:37.140
your interdisciplinary eyes really helped.

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:40.080
I said, "This is an incredibly
clean room for archeology."

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:42.570
And that might not be
enough for archeologist

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:43.860
that's traditionally trained just to stop

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:46.260
and say, "Okay, whatever."

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:47.610
They'll probably overlook it.

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:49.890
But then I saw other clues,
there's like a work bench

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:52.650
with like cutting and pounding tools.

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:55.770
And the floors mean for
bronze age standards,

00:16:55.770 --> 00:16:57.330
that's a very clean room.

00:16:57.330 --> 00:16:58.563
The floor is very clean.

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:03.030
And the vat kind of looked
a little suspicious to me

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:04.920
because there were many
shirts inside of it.

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:06.277
But the director said to me,

00:17:06.277 --> 00:17:07.500
"Andrew, you're wasting your time on this.

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:08.670
It's a wine vat."

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:11.433
In fact, we found some
wine vessels next door,

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:15.930
so why bother with this?

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:19.860
So building up some
goodwill, serving for him,

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:20.910
and so on and so forth, doing things

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:23.797
that he needed me to do, I asked him,

00:17:23.797 --> 00:17:27.000
"It's not gonna cut into
that time, but after hours,

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:30.570
can I collect samples and just for kicks,

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:34.290
take them back to Philadelphia
and then analyze them."

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:36.270
He said, "Sure, as long
as it's on your time."

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:37.170
So on and so forth.

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:39.240
So I never imagined a
dissertation would come out of it.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:41.070
I thought it would just
be interesting to do.

00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:44.350
So I took the samples and
from the get go, he said,

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:46.980
'It's just gonna show that it's wine,

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:50.487
which is, okay, interesting,
but nothing to dwell upon."

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:57.020
So what I did was I really
tried hard to look for wine,

00:17:58.440 --> 00:17:59.670
but again, this is probably,

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:01.710
that's not obviously good science.

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:03.900
You should let the data speak to you.

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:06.060
That was my first lesson
right off the bat.

00:18:06.060 --> 00:18:09.360
And what I found out was
that there was zero evidence

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:12.960
for wine, no tartaric acid,
no citric acid, you know,

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:14.550
malvidin, so on and so forth.

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:16.590
Instead, I found all
these other ingredients,

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:19.650
including trace amounts
of what we would call

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:21.360
aromatics roughly.

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:26.360
And then what became clear is
that it's what has been known

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:30.060
in this field of Minoan
archeology for over a century,

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:32.460
what's called a perfumed oil workshop.

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:35.700
This was a term invented
in the very early days

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:38.370
of archeology there, in the
days of Sir Arthur Evans

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:42.270
and Carl Blegen, these
pioneers of Minoan archeology.

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:47.270
So again, this is a early
lesson in that you need evidence

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:50.880
and insight from many
different perspectives,

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:54.480
because you had the chemical ingredients.

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:56.760
But then we had standing on the shoulder

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:00.570
of these giants who basically discovered

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:03.630
the earliest written
examples of Greek, right?

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:05.910
Called Linear B,

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:09.120
there's these famous
perfumed oil tablets, right?

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:13.230
And that's when this seemed
to coincide with that.

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:16.980
They've never found a
so-called perfume oil workshop.

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:21.980
We know it exists because in
addition to it being written

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:26.460
in these tablets, there are depictions of,

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:30.360
as the wonderful beautiful flyer put out

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:32.460
by the Franke Program.

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:34.320
You might have seen the saffron collector.

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:36.570
So there's examples of
people picking ingredients

00:19:36.570 --> 00:19:39.180
and even, especially in
Egypt, slightly different,

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:41.370
but they're making these
kind of perfumed oils.

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:42.630
So we know about this tradition

00:19:42.630 --> 00:19:44.310
from this exact same time period.

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:46.590
And then I could connect
the dots, extrapolate,

00:19:46.590 --> 00:19:48.360
and then be able to nail down

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:50.970
where these chemical compounds come from.

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:51.990
That's the most difficult thing,

00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:53.340
because there's many options.

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:55.143
You gotta know the local
flora, you gotta know,

00:19:55.143 --> 00:19:56.790
like the chemistry has to make sense,

00:19:56.790 --> 00:20:00.120
the botany has to make
sense, so on and so forth.

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:01.890
So the tablets gave me clues

00:20:01.890 --> 00:20:02.880
what ingredients there might be.

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:06.900
Like coriander is used
as an astringent, right?

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:09.840
Because olive oil doesn't
naturally receive scent.

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:11.580
So I was able to reconstruct

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:13.620
basically how this might all work.

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:15.723
There's all these other clues, right?

00:20:17.370 --> 00:20:19.800
One of the plants is this creticus,

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:23.640
is mentioned by the father of
ancient history, Herodotus.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:26.040
It mentions, you know, in
an ethnographic fashion

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:27.420
what it's all about.

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:32.040
And then related to the flyer
photo, right near there,

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:34.890
there's this depiction that
people always kind of thought

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:37.560
it might be Cretan Rockrose,
it might be another plant.

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:39.810
But again, it's a two-way street.

00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:41.610
By me discovering this,

00:20:41.610 --> 00:20:44.310
then the people doing more
art historical endeavors

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:45.570
are saying we are more confident

00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:48.600
that it's in fact Cistus Creticus.

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:49.680
So that's kind of neat.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:51.090
And going back to my original point,

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:55.007
it's still Cistus Creticus, or
at least a species of Cistus,

00:20:55.007 --> 00:20:57.870
Cistus that grows in
Morocco, believe it or not.

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:01.200
It's still used to make Chanel
No. 5 and things like that.

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:02.970
So I thought that was really interesting.

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:05.490
So I thought it fits,
but you see already here,

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:08.820
in retrospect, I thought
I had to be more careful

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:11.550
because I was in fact also,

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:14.250
it's helpful to have this
previous scholarship,

00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:15.390
but also you can see I was leaning

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:17.850
towards something
aromatic and more cosmetic

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:20.040
because they called it
a perfume oil workshop.

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:21.750
So this is gonna be like lesson two

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:23.343
that I'm gonna find later on.

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:28.320
Because what I found
out is the ingredients

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:29.850
don't quite overlap perfectly,

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:31.710
what we would call a perfume today.

00:21:31.710 --> 00:21:34.260
But you can even see the original scholar,

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:35.760
Cynthia Shelmerdine in '85.

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:37.950
She translated this
linear be a certain way.

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:40.200
This is an example where interpreting it

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:41.250
can be tricky, right?

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:45.180
This isn't something
canonical like, Euripides,

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:46.860
or you know, Virgil,
or something like that.

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:48.780
These are just tablets that
nobody else are gonna read.

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:52.050
So she translated it with
that perfume angle as well,

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:54.630
because that's what people
were saying a hundred years ago

00:21:54.630 --> 00:21:57.750
saying, this is a perfume oil industry.

00:21:57.750 --> 00:22:01.200
But you see, and then
later on, Tom Palaima

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:03.960
who is a MacArthur winner
for his work with Linear B,

00:22:03.960 --> 00:22:06.360
so he's the best of the best, right?

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.043
Translated slightly different.

00:22:09.330 --> 00:22:12.990
But what I would argue is that
he is not a chemist, right?

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:16.230
So for instance, he put down unguent,

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:19.140
which means that it's
solid at room temperature.

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:20.790
And based upon the evidence I've gathered

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:23.580
from not just the site in
East Crete but elsewhere,

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:27.090
it's clear almost certainly
that it was never a fat based,

00:22:27.090 --> 00:22:28.290
you know, or something like that,

00:22:28.290 --> 00:22:30.180
a solid at room temperature.

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:32.640
So even though he might
be the best translator,

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:34.440
Linear B, he's absolutely wrong.

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:36.990
If he understands the technical terms

00:22:36.990 --> 00:22:38.580
as they're defined today.

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:42.030
So again, this might sound
like an exercise in hubris,

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:45.900
but it shows you the power
of interdisciplinarity,

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:48.210
where we work together and
we can inform each other.

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:52.590
So without the science backing
me, I would not do this.

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:54.030
But in essence, I'm saying,
well, there's another way

00:22:54.030 --> 00:22:55.770
to probably translate it, right?

00:22:55.770 --> 00:22:57.510
So I'm trying to veer
us away from perfumes

00:22:57.510 --> 00:22:59.130
because as I'll show you in a second,

00:22:59.130 --> 00:23:04.050
the ingredients suggest,
the evidence suggests,

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:06.180
you know, for instance,
this isn't a normal vat,

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:09.390
you can see this weird spout in front.

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:13.230
And in fact, it's more
pharmacopeic in nature, right?

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:14.760
And it's certainly not a ongoing,

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:16.320
it's something like an ointment,

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:18.360
the literal translation oil boiler.

00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:22.230
And I could say for for
the purpose, it's infusing,

00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:24.330
because of the evidence I've gathered

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:25.860
from this archeological context.

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:28.320
I tested, you know, 80 vessels,

00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:30.060
there's no sign of burning on this vat.

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:31.980
It's clear that the burning is happening

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:34.380
in a room next door
with these cooking pots.

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:36.210
And here that it's kind
of in this big vat,

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:37.980
they're infusing it.

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:42.000
So anyway, the science again,
is informing the translation.

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:45.157
And I met Tom at a conference, I was like,

00:23:45.157 --> 00:23:47.160
"Oh my gosh, he's gonna
say, 'What are you doing?'"

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:48.780
But he said, "No, this
is, keep doing this.

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:51.030
This is incredibly helpful
for people like me,

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:54.120
the science helping the
more humanistic endeavors."

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:56.700
And unless you think
this is a one-off deal.

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:57.690
Another example, again,

00:23:57.690 --> 00:23:59.160
it sounds like an exercise in hubris,

00:23:59.160 --> 00:24:03.093
but I found evidence for
different storax resins.

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:06.480
And in fact, based upon the
description of the father

00:24:06.480 --> 00:24:09.420
of natural history, Pliny the Elder,

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:10.710
writing in the first century,

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:13.110
it's clear that when he
was describing his inferior

00:24:13.110 --> 00:24:15.810
and superior storax in Latin,

00:24:15.810 --> 00:24:17.310
that he's actually almost
certainly describing

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:19.170
two different species, right?

00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:22.560
Things like that, so I actually
went back to the Pliny,

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:25.710
and told my Latinous
friend that he actually,

00:24:25.710 --> 00:24:27.780
it's not even that people
are translating wrong

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:30.540
in the modern day, but actually
Pliny is misunderstanding it

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:32.670
because he has different sources.

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:34.350
He's presuming it's from the same plant

00:24:34.350 --> 00:24:35.910
and it's just better qualities,

00:24:35.910 --> 00:24:38.250
but in fact they're coming
from different plants.

00:24:38.250 --> 00:24:40.770
So that's the kind of
information we can give.

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:43.650
Why did I start veering
more towards pharmacology?

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:47.340
Because in this very same
perfumed oil workshop,

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:49.743
there were copious
amounts of ferulic acid.

00:24:50.670 --> 00:24:52.680
And at first you can think it comes from,

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:53.910
you know, giant fennel.

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:56.550
But again, that's not really
aromatic of that sort.

00:24:56.550 --> 00:24:59.010
And it doesn't really have a property

00:24:59.010 --> 00:25:01.200
like coriander to be an astringent.

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:03.930
In fact, it's really
either if you're going

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:05.190
with giant fennel, it's culinary,

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:07.410
and this, it definitely isn't a kitchen.

00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:10.570
So then all of a sudden, what
I thought about is the fact

00:25:11.430 --> 00:25:15.240
that other species of
ferula, besides giant fennel,

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:17.220
have very pharmacological properties

00:25:17.220 --> 00:25:20.703
that are still front and center
in this region of the world.

00:25:22.110 --> 00:25:25.050
On top of that, this is where
the transdisciplinary approach

00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:29.400
comes in, there's a society
in modern day now Libya,

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:32.580
the entire city state was
founded on this one commodity

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:34.500
they had, called Silphium.

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:37.290
It was so important that they
harvested it to extinction.

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:40.440
So we can talk about ancient ecology now.

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:42.930
The last example supposedly was presented

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:44.820
to the emperor Nero as a curiosity

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:46.500
because it was the last example.

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:49.800
And they had, you know,
leveraged it to extinction.

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:51.930
So this society, their coins were based

00:25:51.930 --> 00:25:53.283
upon this ferula plant,

00:25:54.510 --> 00:25:57.180
and it was more valuable than gold.

00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:58.530
And the fascinating thing about is,

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:00.420
it's one of those instances where Pliny

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:04.440
is usually very verbose
about and specific about

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:05.640
the purpose of these plants

00:26:05.640 --> 00:26:07.260
to the point of being speculative.

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:09.300
But what's fascinating is he
doesn't really talk too much

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:11.880
about it, as you can see in this quote.

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:13.890
So you gotta do other research.

00:26:13.890 --> 00:26:16.800
And what you find out is when
they're referencing Silphium,

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:19.297
like Avid, another author
says something like,

00:26:19.297 --> 00:26:22.230
"If those two keep it up, they're
gonna need some Silphium."

00:26:22.230 --> 00:26:26.660
Right, so what we realized
based upon acid found

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:28.470
in the modern day, so and so forth,

00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:31.410
and other studies done
in the last centuries,

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:35.490
people have claimed it's in,
I believe it's probably in,

00:26:35.490 --> 00:26:38.493
at least in human history
the strongest abortifacient,

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:41.070
right, in quantities enough,

00:26:41.070 --> 00:26:42.600
unless you think I'm being speculative,

00:26:42.600 --> 00:26:47.280
because Cyrene occurs some centuries later

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:49.170
than the workshop I studied.

00:26:49.170 --> 00:26:53.730
In the room next door to
the perfume old workshop,

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:56.400
we found a seal.

00:26:56.400 --> 00:26:59.940
And it was, this was excavated,
not in recent memory,

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:02.400
but by the original
excavator, Richard Seager,

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:04.080
in the early 20th century.

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:06.540
And people were like, "What
is this seal doing here?"

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:08.340
Because it's a silphium seed.

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:10.560
So it raised all these questions,

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:14.580
Silphium wasn't really supposed
to be used for, you know,

00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:16.770
until centuries later
from this time period.

00:27:16.770 --> 00:27:19.070
Silphium's only supposed
to grow in Cyrenaica,

00:27:20.490 --> 00:27:22.920
the modern coast of Libya.

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:23.940
So there are all these questions,

00:27:23.940 --> 00:27:26.550
but all of a sudden the, so
this makes me more confident

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:29.250
that there might have
been some kind of Silphium

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:33.240
used in this workshop because
of this additional evidence.

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:37.050
And on top of that, I think
it also opens the possibility

00:27:37.050 --> 00:27:41.160
that this isn't just simply
an aromatic cosmetic facility.

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:44.220
So then I started to rethink
how to approach this.

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:47.133
So with those lessons I
learned around 20 years ago,

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:51.090
you know, just fast
forwarding over a lot of years

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:52.860
of trying to figure this all out,

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:54.460
that led me here to New Haven

00:27:55.350 --> 00:27:57.120
and the Yale Ancient Pharmacology Program.

00:27:57.120 --> 00:27:59.130
But you can see the pillars of my research

00:27:59.130 --> 00:28:02.100
are based upon that original work

00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:04.590
of approaching it from different angles,

00:28:04.590 --> 00:28:07.770
relying upon a more
social science approach,

00:28:07.770 --> 00:28:10.320
and ethnography, the science
and then the technology,

00:28:10.320 --> 00:28:12.330
and also including the
ethnography, of course,

00:28:12.330 --> 00:28:14.610
are the humanistic approaches with texts.

00:28:14.610 --> 00:28:16.710
And you can see being
based at the Peabody Museum

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:19.380
is a fantastic thing, because
I could walk down the hall,

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:21.330
I could talk to Patrick Sweeney,

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:25.260
reference the wonderful herbarium there.

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:29.130
I could walk down to Mineralogy,
talk about petrography

00:28:29.130 --> 00:28:30.360
and all those other kind of things.

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:33.930
And of course, the vast
anthropological division as well

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:35.430
with this millions of objects,

00:28:35.430 --> 00:28:36.780
I could start studying these things.

00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:38.190
And as you can see in the lower right,

00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:39.990
these wonderful students

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:41.550
coming from all kinds of backgrounds,

00:28:41.550 --> 00:28:45.780
I mean biochemistry to Classics, to NELC

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:47.610
and everything else in between.

00:28:47.610 --> 00:28:49.590
So working with them to try to figure out

00:28:49.590 --> 00:28:50.793
some of these mysteries.

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:55.980
How do I go about doing this data

00:28:55.980 --> 00:28:57.000
is really the most important thing,

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:59.430
as you can imagine from
all different angles.

00:28:59.430 --> 00:29:02.280
And we need robust data,

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:04.800
kind of a two-pronged approach.

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:08.460
Both legacy collections, which
is abundant here at Yale.

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:10.890
Millions of objects just
waiting to be studied.

00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:13.650
As we were just talking about earlier on.

00:29:13.650 --> 00:29:18.650
There are, I mean untold
amounts of pottery

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:21.390
from Dura-Europos,
never will be displayed.

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:26.390
They're not pretty, but
they're incredibly interesting

00:29:26.730 --> 00:29:28.590
to my eyes, because they're still

00:29:28.590 --> 00:29:30.330
in their original bags all dirty.

00:29:30.330 --> 00:29:32.130
That's what I need to
get the organic sample.

00:29:32.130 --> 00:29:34.710
So it's gonna be overlooked
by most art historians,

00:29:34.710 --> 00:29:36.270
probably, if not all.

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:39.123
But then there's incredible
data in there for me.

00:29:40.620 --> 00:29:42.360
But you also need new field work, right?

00:29:42.360 --> 00:29:46.080
Especially things like
ethnobotany, so on and so forth.

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:47.850
So we kind of have that approach,

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:49.110
and we have these collaborations.

00:29:49.110 --> 00:29:52.470
So in the title I mentioned cross campus.

00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:55.920
One of the nodes of research
is my ethnohistory team.

00:29:55.920 --> 00:29:58.950
And we work closely with the
Yale Babylonian collection.

00:29:58.950 --> 00:30:00.750
And this is an example of a mystery

00:30:00.750 --> 00:30:02.243
that we've somewhat solved.

00:30:02.243 --> 00:30:06.330
I mean, this is a vessel
from the days of JP Morgan

00:30:06.330 --> 00:30:11.330
inscribed with the great
King of Persia, Xerxes.

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:13.680
And people could have
speculated that, you know,

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:15.690
where they're from,
they're exceedingly rare

00:30:15.690 --> 00:30:18.030
and only found the most elite of context.

00:30:18.030 --> 00:30:21.540
The tomb of Mausolus, the
namesake of Mausoleums

00:30:21.540 --> 00:30:22.373
so on and so forth.

00:30:22.373 --> 00:30:24.060
And the Yale Babylonian collection

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:28.020
has an amazing intact
piece that's on display now

00:30:28.020 --> 00:30:31.680
or will be soon in the
new exhibit at the museum.

00:30:31.680 --> 00:30:34.710
We did these studies and they
contain opiates, all right?

00:30:34.710 --> 00:30:37.800
So we can start thinking
what they're doing here.

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:39.900
And we can kind of extrapolate that.

00:30:39.900 --> 00:30:43.290
So, and King Tut's tomb, this
was a mystery never solved,

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:44.820
but people don't realize
that King Tut's tomb

00:30:44.820 --> 00:30:45.810
was looted twice.

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:49.050
The first time it was looted,
they took barely anything,

00:30:49.050 --> 00:30:50.430
we think some medals,

00:30:50.430 --> 00:30:53.580
things that are portable
and then they fled.

00:30:53.580 --> 00:30:58.580
The second looting incident
was much more systematic.

00:31:00.060 --> 00:31:02.520
They left all the portable jewels

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:05.160
and gold and things like that.

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:08.610
In fact, you know, what they took was,

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:12.630
this is the vessel you
see here is maybe like

00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:16.413
the volume of a soda can,
something like 330 milliliters,

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:21.690
these vast pharaonic
versions of this vessel,

00:31:21.690 --> 00:31:24.060
right at the height of
ancient Egypt power.

00:31:24.060 --> 00:31:27.570
There are dozens and dozens
of these calcite jars

00:31:27.570 --> 00:31:30.630
that was filled with the
same kind of brown substance.

00:31:30.630 --> 00:31:33.570
And that second looting
incident, all but one of these,

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:36.210
two of these jars, they
put them into sacks.

00:31:36.210 --> 00:31:38.610
You can see still the finger marks

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:39.957
from these large calcite vessels.

00:31:39.957 --> 00:31:41.730
And we know they put them in sacks

00:31:41.730 --> 00:31:44.460
because they looted it so
hastily, they left one of them.

00:31:44.460 --> 00:31:46.170
And there are remnants of this sack

00:31:46.170 --> 00:31:48.360
with this brown stuff in it.

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:51.420
So never studied, and
part of the narrative

00:31:51.420 --> 00:31:53.520
of King Tut's tomb that's
never been talked about, right?

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:56.420
The fact that of all the
treasures they could have stolen,

00:31:57.870 --> 00:32:00.300
more than the first
slight looting incident,

00:32:00.300 --> 00:32:03.240
their main concern was
getting probably opiates.

00:32:03.240 --> 00:32:04.353
So fascinating.

00:32:05.460 --> 00:32:08.910
We're also working with
the archeology department

00:32:08.910 --> 00:32:11.220
because what we do, since
it's about landscapes,

00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:13.830
also the ecology, so we
have these wonderful people,

00:32:13.830 --> 00:32:15.720
the Yale Center for Earth Observation

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:18.150
is helping us doing
multi-spectral analysis.

00:32:18.150 --> 00:32:19.560
And that's, I'll talk
about more in a second.

00:32:19.560 --> 00:32:22.440
There's an island off the
coast here owned by the museum.

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:24.510
And it allows us to do all these
kind of interesting studies

00:32:24.510 --> 00:32:26.250
that are trying to push the envelope

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:28.743
of the data we could get.

00:32:30.570 --> 00:32:32.613
But there's a parallel plan, like I said,

00:32:32.613 --> 00:32:34.500
with the field work.

00:32:34.500 --> 00:32:39.500
So we're working with the Thebes
Museum, they're wonderful.

00:32:39.600 --> 00:32:43.320
George Syrimis in the
lower right is the Director

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:44.520
of the Yale Hellenic Studies Program.

00:32:44.520 --> 00:32:45.630
So working with him as well,

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:47.520
because again, there's
this ethnographic component

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:49.740
that he's been incredibly helpful with.

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:52.590
And working with the locals hand in hand.

00:32:52.590 --> 00:32:56.940
And then we have for instance,
at the botany angle here

00:32:56.940 --> 00:32:59.970
at the Yale Herbarium, as I
mentioned Patrick Sweeney.

00:32:59.970 --> 00:33:04.320
I mean there are specimens
from over 150 years ago

00:33:04.320 --> 00:33:07.650
collected by the founder of the herbarium.

00:33:07.650 --> 00:33:11.970
So wonderful access to things
like here, very early grapes.

00:33:11.970 --> 00:33:15.420
And there's, again, there's
a para loan in Greece

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:17.400
where with the locals we're
collecting the very plants

00:33:17.400 --> 00:33:19.530
mentioned by these ancient authors

00:33:19.530 --> 00:33:21.960
that were supposed to cure
all kinds of different things.

00:33:21.960 --> 00:33:25.260
Absolutely fascinating,
so for instance, here,

00:33:25.260 --> 00:33:28.627
Helleborus Niger, the
individual there, he said,

00:33:28.627 --> 00:33:31.320
"Well, for you guys, this
is one species to you.

00:33:31.320 --> 00:33:34.080
But the way we've learned
it, and I believe it is,

00:33:34.080 --> 00:33:37.290
we have seven different
valleys where we've categorized

00:33:37.290 --> 00:33:38.820
seven different versions of these plants."

00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:40.620
I mean, he's not saying it's a subspecies

00:33:40.620 --> 00:33:42.960
or anything like that, but
clearly as they understand that,

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:45.510
they call it the local
ecologies are that specific,

00:33:45.510 --> 00:33:46.830
the soil, so on and so forth.

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:48.840
So we're starting to investigate
these kind of things.

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:50.970
I mean, there are legends written

00:33:50.970 --> 00:33:53.250
by these ancient historians
about these plants.

00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:55.620
So we're starting to try to
get to the bottom of this.

00:33:55.620 --> 00:33:57.630
And having the medical
historical library here

00:33:57.630 --> 00:33:58.950
is absolutely amazing.

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:01.920
They have one of less than
50 facsimiles of what I think

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:04.800
is probably my favorite ancient document.

00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:08.220
The Vienna Dioscorides,
the Juliana Anicia Codex,

00:34:08.220 --> 00:34:11.283
the original, of course is in Vienna,

00:34:12.210 --> 00:34:16.350
and it was written 1,500 years ago for,

00:34:16.350 --> 00:34:18.690
you can't make this stuff
up, for a Roman princess

00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:21.090
who then got married into
the power of the time,

00:34:21.090 --> 00:34:23.940
the Byzantine Empire,
and that royal family.

00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:26.670
She brought it to
Constantinople, modern Istanbul,

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:28.860
where sometime, you
know, during her lifetime

00:34:28.860 --> 00:34:30.750
or shortly afterwards, she gifted it

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:33.540
to the Imperial Hospital
in Constantinople.

00:34:33.540 --> 00:34:35.520
And most important for my research,

00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:39.840
then for a thousand years
plus, it was used as a guide

00:34:39.840 --> 00:34:42.870
for actual practitioners of medicine,

00:34:42.870 --> 00:34:44.400
pharmacy, so on and so forth.

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:46.980
Eventually given to the Sultans,

00:34:46.980 --> 00:34:49.290
after the Ottomans took
over Constantinople,

00:34:49.290 --> 00:34:51.780
the Sultan's Jewish physician who then,

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:54.840
and then after him, it
got gifted to I think

00:34:54.840 --> 00:34:57.690
a Flemish diplomat who gifted
it to the Holy Roman emperor.

00:34:57.690 --> 00:34:59.040
That's how it got to Vienna.

00:34:59.040 --> 00:35:01.590
But you can see here in
the right, it's remarkable

00:35:01.590 --> 00:35:04.113
because that center portion,

00:35:05.130 --> 00:35:08.310
so this portion is the
original first century text

00:35:08.310 --> 00:35:10.560
of De Materia Medica, right?

00:35:10.560 --> 00:35:13.950
Dioscorides, the author himself,
has an incredible story,

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:15.360
which I won't get into here,

00:35:15.360 --> 00:35:17.820
but you can see all the different hands.

00:35:17.820 --> 00:35:20.433
It's not, so the original
text is Byzantine Greek.

00:35:21.420 --> 00:35:22.590
A lot of the Scholia is Greek's

00:35:22.590 --> 00:35:24.300
that was used in Constantinople.

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:28.380
But you'll also know there
is a scholia marginalia

00:35:28.380 --> 00:35:32.073
in Turkish, Arabic, Hebrew,

00:35:33.357 --> 00:35:35.730
a slew of others, Persian.

00:35:35.730 --> 00:35:38.010
And in fact, one of my students
who's looking at analysis,

00:35:38.010 --> 00:35:41.910
there's actually a Germanic,
or very early Germanic

00:35:41.910 --> 00:35:45.720
writing on it, probably done in Vienna.

00:35:45.720 --> 00:35:47.550
So it's absolutely astounding.

00:35:47.550 --> 00:35:51.450
And there really is no
translations of this scholia.

00:35:51.450 --> 00:35:54.463
My students love this because
the closest example is,

00:35:56.910 --> 00:35:58.980
again, it's fiction is really

00:35:58.980 --> 00:36:01.080
the Harry Potter potions manual.

00:36:01.080 --> 00:36:02.790
So if you guys know that story, right?

00:36:02.790 --> 00:36:05.340
The value was not really,
if you know the narrative,

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:07.710
it's not in the original text,

00:36:07.710 --> 00:36:10.410
but it's with Professor
Severus Snape, right?

00:36:10.410 --> 00:36:12.607
As I understand it,
putting all these notes in,

00:36:12.607 --> 00:36:13.860
"This is how you cut this plant.

00:36:13.860 --> 00:36:15.810
Don't use that plant,
use the side of a knife."

00:36:15.810 --> 00:36:19.020
And that's exactly what the
Vienna Dioscorides is all about.

00:36:19.020 --> 00:36:20.700
And we're just starting
to plumb those depths

00:36:20.700 --> 00:36:22.050
because it falls through the cracks.

00:36:22.050 --> 00:36:24.300
Who's gonna study it, is a
botanist that's gonna study it?

00:36:24.300 --> 00:36:25.800
Is the Classics that's gonna study it?

00:36:25.800 --> 00:36:28.140
Is a NELC scholar gonna study it?

00:36:28.140 --> 00:36:31.050
The fact of matter is, and I
can still relate to this book,

00:36:31.050 --> 00:36:33.600
it very much falls through the cracks

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:35.130
and we're just starting to explore it.

00:36:35.130 --> 00:36:38.340
For instance, as the
herbarium director told me,

00:36:38.340 --> 00:36:41.430
it was made for a
princess, it's so accurate.

00:36:41.430 --> 00:36:44.940
The illustrations, you
can identify species.

00:36:44.940 --> 00:36:47.130
So all of a sudden these,
again, going back to translate,

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:49.710
we don't know what plant
this is in the Linear B text,

00:36:49.710 --> 00:36:51.660
all of a sudden we can say, "Well,

00:36:51.660 --> 00:36:54.270
at least in this time
period, we know what species,

00:36:54.270 --> 00:36:55.920
we can identify the species of plant

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:57.360
and we know what the Greek says.

00:36:57.360 --> 00:36:59.160
So at least we can put in that moment.

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:00.240
This was their understanding

00:37:00.240 --> 00:37:01.500
of how they identified the plant."

00:37:01.500 --> 00:37:03.750
First time ever, but nobody's really,

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:05.040
maybe someone's thought about doing it,

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:07.140
but you can see the difficulties in fact

00:37:07.140 --> 00:37:10.200
of actually putting the
expertise together to do it.

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:11.850
When I asked the Medical
Historical Library,

00:37:11.850 --> 00:37:13.740
how does this book exist here?

00:37:13.740 --> 00:37:15.690
Because there's five
books in the original,

00:37:15.690 --> 00:37:20.180
it's exquisite, accurate,
facsimile made in 1970

00:37:20.180 --> 00:37:22.470
to the point that you can still
see the body fingerprints,

00:37:22.470 --> 00:37:25.650
it's the exact same
size, so on and so forth.

00:37:25.650 --> 00:37:28.920
It took separate library
endowments to buy each book.

00:37:28.920 --> 00:37:31.290
I can't imagine what the cost was in 1970,

00:37:31.290 --> 00:37:34.200
but it surprised me that four
of the books were never opened

00:37:34.200 --> 00:37:36.120
and then you still have
the little, you know,

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:38.520
endowed by this family or that
family was never attached.

00:37:38.520 --> 00:37:40.717
So I asked the staff there, like,

00:37:40.717 --> 00:37:42.690
"Why did you buy this,
and who is this for?"

00:37:42.690 --> 00:37:43.800
And then they paused for a second.

00:37:43.800 --> 00:37:46.887
They said, "Apparently it
was purchased for you."

00:37:46.887 --> 00:37:49.170
And that was a really nice sentiment.

00:37:49.170 --> 00:37:50.313
It only took 50 years.

00:37:51.180 --> 00:37:52.410
I'm glad they purchased it.

00:37:52.410 --> 00:37:55.050
So again, we're connecting
to these oral histories.

00:37:55.050 --> 00:37:56.850
We're working with the local people,

00:37:58.260 --> 00:38:01.350
and we're working towards
the first purpose-built

00:38:01.350 --> 00:38:02.580
lab for ancient pharmacology.

00:38:02.580 --> 00:38:05.760
So this is a photo of our
pedator in the basement

00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:07.410
of the museum right now.

00:38:07.410 --> 00:38:09.690
Great space, but we actually want to make,

00:38:09.690 --> 00:38:12.570
we're renovating a 1600
square foot modular lab

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:15.690
in West campus where all this
can happen in one location.

00:38:15.690 --> 00:38:16.950
And again, this wouldn't happen

00:38:16.950 --> 00:38:18.210
without the wonderful students.

00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:21.660
So Ria Singh was a first year student

00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:26.280
in the collection of the
Peabody Museum seminar.

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:28.680
And she gamely joined the team

00:38:28.680 --> 00:38:32.010
and she's doing this wonderful
work, ethno botanical work

00:38:32.010 --> 00:38:34.410
with another mystery we solved.

00:38:34.410 --> 00:38:35.700
It's a whole other talk,

00:38:35.700 --> 00:38:38.190
but we solved this mystery
of what this installation

00:38:38.190 --> 00:38:41.670
in what is now the border of
Egypt and Sudan was all about.

00:38:41.670 --> 00:38:43.710
It was a wine installation.

00:38:43.710 --> 00:38:46.650
And she's helping me look
at the earlier plants.

00:38:46.650 --> 00:38:50.580
I showed you the
specimens of the different

00:38:50.580 --> 00:38:53.160
vidas plants in the herbarium.

00:38:53.160 --> 00:38:55.140
And we're connecting those dots.

00:38:55.140 --> 00:38:59.910
So, as I kind of happily say,

00:38:59.910 --> 00:39:02.820
if a student joins my lab,
they're certainly gonna work,

00:39:02.820 --> 00:39:07.743
you know, with the
primary research mission.

00:39:09.690 --> 00:39:11.910
And then, our last angle, of course,

00:39:11.910 --> 00:39:15.360
is the technological angle
because it's a ton of data.

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:18.630
How do you deal with terabytes
and terabytes of information?

00:39:18.630 --> 00:39:21.300
And of course, right
in house at the museum

00:39:21.300 --> 00:39:22.740
we have the informatics division

00:39:22.740 --> 00:39:24.960
led by Nelson Rios and others.

00:39:24.960 --> 00:39:27.930
So it's just been a wonderful luxury

00:39:27.930 --> 00:39:30.600
to have all these specialists helping us.

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:32.970
Just very quickly, the
way I want to end is

00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:35.070
I do want to cite this challenge

00:39:35.070 --> 00:39:37.080
that we're facing in case there are

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:39.630
archeologists in the room,
aspiring archeologists.

00:39:39.630 --> 00:39:43.380
We're dealing with 150 year plus tradition

00:39:43.380 --> 00:39:45.120
of how to do archeology.

00:39:45.120 --> 00:39:47.040
So this is from just two looted tombs.

00:39:47.040 --> 00:39:49.620
We need to get data, but the same time,

00:39:49.620 --> 00:39:51.690
the way it works now, for better or worse

00:39:51.690 --> 00:39:53.850
is that you wanna find spectacular
things typically, right?

00:39:53.850 --> 00:39:57.030
To get grants, you wanna find
palaces, so on and so forth.

00:39:57.030 --> 00:39:59.250
The way that we frame the
project now, the program,

00:39:59.250 --> 00:40:01.500
it allows us the luxury
of being more strategic.

00:40:01.500 --> 00:40:03.060
Because if you wanna do this right,

00:40:03.060 --> 00:40:06.570
this is just from one rescued tomb.

00:40:06.570 --> 00:40:09.750
You can imagine how many millions
of shirbs are coming out.

00:40:09.750 --> 00:40:11.850
This wasn't even our rescue excavation.

00:40:11.850 --> 00:40:13.380
That's from a previous rescue

00:40:13.380 --> 00:40:15.210
and they're asking us to study it.

00:40:15.210 --> 00:40:19.280
So if you indiscriminately do archeology

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:21.390
as traditionally done,

00:40:21.390 --> 00:40:23.730
we're talking about decades of commitment.

00:40:23.730 --> 00:40:26.940
And so we've tried to think
about different way to do it.

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:29.790
So one way to do it differently
is, rather than showing up,

00:40:29.790 --> 00:40:31.650
you know, all jet lagged in Greece

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:33.630
and just taking days to set up,

00:40:33.630 --> 00:40:36.090
we train our students on
Horse Island, as I mentioned,

00:40:36.090 --> 00:40:39.510
and they do exactly everything
we would do in Greece,

00:40:39.510 --> 00:40:43.140
from the botanical, you know, specimens

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:45.270
to the UAV techniques.

00:40:45.270 --> 00:40:49.410
So they were surprised this past summer,

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:50.880
the first time we did this is,

00:40:50.880 --> 00:40:52.980
because our students
hit the ground running.

00:40:52.980 --> 00:40:54.660
They knew exactly what to do

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:56.820
and it was the best compliment

00:40:56.820 --> 00:40:59.047
that the local authorities gave us.

00:40:59.047 --> 00:41:01.470
"How are they just on the fly doing this?

00:41:01.470 --> 00:41:03.360
There's usually like a training

00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:04.530
process, a feeling out process."

00:41:04.530 --> 00:41:07.830
And I explained to them
this wonderful resource

00:41:07.830 --> 00:41:10.547
that the Peabody Museum has
provided to save us that time.

00:41:10.547 --> 00:41:12.810
'Cause many of our students
have internships to go to.

00:41:12.810 --> 00:41:14.610
They have senators to intern for,

00:41:14.610 --> 00:41:16.890
they wanna go work at the
London School of Economics

00:41:16.890 --> 00:41:21.540
or Stern, so we squeeze this
in, in a very tight season,

00:41:21.540 --> 00:41:24.240
typically less than a
month in May and June,

00:41:24.240 --> 00:41:26.633
before they have to go
off to their internships.

00:41:27.870 --> 00:41:30.180
So just very quickly, this
is really another talk

00:41:30.180 --> 00:41:32.670
for another time, and I
wanna wrap up here soon,

00:41:32.670 --> 00:41:35.790
but these are just the goals
that, for this audience,

00:41:35.790 --> 00:41:38.190
for the most part it will
seem pretty innocuous.

00:41:38.190 --> 00:41:41.790
But when archeologists typically see this,

00:41:41.790 --> 00:41:44.730
I presented this at the
Archeological Institute of America

00:41:44.730 --> 00:41:46.560
conference early this month.

00:41:46.560 --> 00:41:50.737
And people said on one hand,

00:41:50.737 --> 00:41:52.830
"I can't believe you're
doing this, I have to do it."

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:54.487
And on the other hand, they were saying,

00:41:54.487 --> 00:41:56.160
"This has been a long time coming

00:41:56.160 --> 00:41:57.690
because we need a sustainable model

00:41:57.690 --> 00:42:00.030
in terms of doing archeology."

00:42:00.030 --> 00:42:01.350
How do we do it in C2?

00:42:01.350 --> 00:42:04.260
How do we do it without
having millions of objects

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:06.480
sitting in our collections gathering dust,

00:42:06.480 --> 00:42:07.980
not looked at for a century?

00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:12.980
So prioritizing this
approach, being very targeted,

00:42:12.990 --> 00:42:17.550
looking at microclimates in
collaboration with the locals,

00:42:17.550 --> 00:42:19.770
and finally making it sustainable,

00:42:19.770 --> 00:42:21.120
which is a very tall order.

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:25.320
So just to sum up now, time
for some glamour shots,

00:42:25.320 --> 00:42:28.350
if you'll, you know, amuse me.

00:42:28.350 --> 00:42:30.600
So believe it or not,
this is not something

00:42:30.600 --> 00:42:32.640
that we kind of front load,

00:42:32.640 --> 00:42:36.870
because maybe in a more
traditional dig they would do it.

00:42:36.870 --> 00:42:40.320
It's kind of cool, we're
digging Homeric sites, right?

00:42:40.320 --> 00:42:43.260
If you guys, most of you probably
of course know the Iliad,

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:45.180
but these are somewhat obscure sites,

00:42:45.180 --> 00:42:48.150
but we're confident that we've identified

00:42:48.150 --> 00:42:50.133
these two sites correctly.

00:42:51.480 --> 00:42:55.290
So it's in this region of
southern focus we're, I'm the PI,

00:42:55.290 --> 00:42:58.080
it's near the ancient side of
Delphi, but it's overlooked

00:42:58.080 --> 00:43:00.870
because it's overshadowed
by this classical,

00:43:00.870 --> 00:43:05.220
very famous classical
sanctuary of the oracle there.

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:10.220
But we want to go before even
Delphi was really even a site.

00:43:11.280 --> 00:43:12.930
This was centuries before.

00:43:12.930 --> 00:43:15.063
So the Homeric, the Bronze Age period.

00:43:15.960 --> 00:43:20.960
And it's cloaked in legend
and all kinds of myths.

00:43:21.780 --> 00:43:25.590
I mean Mount Helicon,
the land of the Muses

00:43:25.590 --> 00:43:26.940
where I collected that
hellebore, by the way,

00:43:26.940 --> 00:43:28.650
which is so cool.

00:43:28.650 --> 00:43:31.020
And of course Delphi is nearby,

00:43:31.020 --> 00:43:33.930
but also the site that we're
gonna work out work at.

00:43:33.930 --> 00:43:37.350
That's where Mark Anthony
mustered his troops and got grain

00:43:37.350 --> 00:43:41.460
before he fought at Actium with Cleopatra.

00:43:41.460 --> 00:43:44.970
I mean, crazy things have
happened here, the histories.

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:48.120
So this is nearby is where
King Philip and Alexander

00:43:48.120 --> 00:43:52.860
subdued the local populace
to become the rulers

00:43:52.860 --> 00:43:55.470
of the ancient Greek world and
start the Hellenistic period

00:43:55.470 --> 00:43:58.560
all the way to South
Asia, so on and so forth.

00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:00.933
So incredible history
that we're working at.

00:44:02.280 --> 00:44:05.760
So you can see our site
up, that's Mount Parnassus,

00:44:05.760 --> 00:44:08.463
another very famous
mountain in the region,

00:44:09.750 --> 00:44:13.060
kind of like the start
of the Balkans there.

00:44:13.060 --> 00:44:16.680
We're at that zone, a very sleepy village.

00:44:16.680 --> 00:44:19.950
No tour buses come here,
unlike very nearby,

00:44:19.950 --> 00:44:23.520
Delphi is just off here to
the side across the valley.

00:44:23.520 --> 00:44:25.230
And this is the site most
of you probably know,

00:44:25.230 --> 00:44:26.730
many of you have probably
even been to Delphi.

00:44:26.730 --> 00:44:29.643
Our side is this mountain
range, so across this valley,

00:44:32.130 --> 00:44:33.990
but we're concentrating on this area

00:44:33.990 --> 00:44:35.700
because of a couple reasons.

00:44:35.700 --> 00:44:40.413
One, it's overlooked, but
this is where a lot of the,

00:44:41.340 --> 00:44:43.320
from, I mean it's a cool story,

00:44:43.320 --> 00:44:44.820
I won't get into it too much,

00:44:44.820 --> 00:44:48.000
but for instance, Hippocrates's
ancestor supposedly

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:51.360
used the hellebore to
subdue a nearby rival town

00:44:51.360 --> 00:44:53.850
by contaminating their water source.

00:44:53.850 --> 00:44:56.700
And apparently they all
got dysentery and weakened

00:44:56.700 --> 00:44:59.490
and they were able to subdue
this neighboring city state.

00:44:59.490 --> 00:45:04.490
So the legend goes, his
descendant was so despondent

00:45:04.920 --> 00:45:06.697
about this family history, he said,

00:45:06.697 --> 00:45:07.890
"I gotta do something about,"

00:45:07.890 --> 00:45:09.540
and you guys can probably
guess what that is.

00:45:09.540 --> 00:45:11.576
It's the Hippocratic Oath of doing,

00:45:11.576 --> 00:45:14.123
I mean this is, I mean it's,

00:45:14.123 --> 00:45:16.710
this is like a brief many
layers of history here.

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:18.330
It's absolutely amazing.

00:45:18.330 --> 00:45:21.600
So this is the town, there's
a entire Roman city underneath

00:45:21.600 --> 00:45:26.310
of Antikyra, but we are not
concentrating down so much.

00:45:26.310 --> 00:45:29.670
But the periphery incredible stuff.

00:45:29.670 --> 00:45:32.370
This is just picking stuff
off the ground at our site.

00:45:33.270 --> 00:45:35.880
It both excites us and scares us,

00:45:35.880 --> 00:45:38.370
because if this is coming out the surface,

00:45:38.370 --> 00:45:41.250
then we know that there's
tremendous stuff under the ground.

00:45:41.250 --> 00:45:43.890
And that requires,
again, time and expertise

00:45:43.890 --> 00:45:45.990
that we have to be very careful about

00:45:45.990 --> 00:45:48.990
because that might take us
away from our main focus.

00:45:48.990 --> 00:45:51.727
As crazy as it sounds,
the authorities are like,

00:45:51.727 --> 00:45:52.980
"We've never met people like you

00:45:52.980 --> 00:45:54.330
because typically you guys just wanna dig

00:45:54.330 --> 00:45:55.680
the mega on and get all the gold.

00:45:55.680 --> 00:45:57.774
So you guys are very strange

00:45:57.774 --> 00:46:00.150
and very interesting at the same time."

00:46:00.150 --> 00:46:01.380
I take that as a compliment.

00:46:01.380 --> 00:46:04.110
I mean, we're finding
bronze pins on the surface.

00:46:04.110 --> 00:46:05.880
I mean, it's incredible.

00:46:05.880 --> 00:46:10.880
So I just wanna do this
quick view of the site,

00:46:11.880 --> 00:46:13.173
obviously very beautiful.

00:46:14.340 --> 00:46:15.930
You guys are welcome to visit.

00:46:15.930 --> 00:46:18.360
I know it's probably not gonna be much,

00:46:18.360 --> 00:46:19.440
take much to convince.

00:46:19.440 --> 00:46:22.983
It's a Homeric site, it's
fortified, incredible harbor.

00:46:25.350 --> 00:46:27.780
But this is Greece, it's just,

00:46:27.780 --> 00:46:29.550
I know people look at the greatest hits,

00:46:29.550 --> 00:46:32.433
the Athenian Acropolis,
so on on and so forth.

00:46:34.560 --> 00:46:36.210
That's one of the locals,
not one of our students.

00:46:36.210 --> 00:46:38.973
Our students are down
here collecting pottery,

00:46:39.810 --> 00:46:42.570
but people always say
very "Lord of the Rings."

00:46:42.570 --> 00:46:44.913
Never excavated, and it's a Homeric site.

00:46:47.880 --> 00:46:49.080
We're doing geomorphology.

00:46:49.080 --> 00:46:51.317
This was a peninsula at the time,

00:46:51.317 --> 00:46:53.640
but there was probably
water all along this area.

00:46:53.640 --> 00:46:55.530
There's still a perennial stream here,

00:46:55.530 --> 00:46:57.180
which is very unusual for Greece.

00:46:58.170 --> 00:47:00.843
So, you know, the water
source was tremendous.

00:47:01.770 --> 00:47:03.570
And then just very quickly, the work

00:47:03.570 --> 00:47:06.780
that the wonderful students
are doing, this is a 3D model

00:47:06.780 --> 00:47:09.210
with such accuracy we can
see shirbs on the ground.

00:47:09.210 --> 00:47:11.753
Alright, so again, trying
to do things differently.

00:47:12.930 --> 00:47:14.760
And finally, I want to kind of end

00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:17.400
by pointing out again the history of this.

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:20.850
There's a very famous second century

00:47:20.850 --> 00:47:25.620
Greek Roman, Romanized Greek, Pausanias.

00:47:25.620 --> 00:47:27.180
He kind of wrote the first,

00:47:27.180 --> 00:47:28.770
I don't know what the
modern equivalent is,

00:47:28.770 --> 00:47:30.510
is someone in the audience can tell me,

00:47:30.510 --> 00:47:31.560
I used to say blue guide,

00:47:31.560 --> 00:47:33.930
but students don't know what
a blue guide is anymore.

00:47:33.930 --> 00:47:37.908
So TripAdvisor maybe, he
basically travels the countryside

00:47:37.908 --> 00:47:39.540
of Greece in the second century

00:47:39.540 --> 00:47:41.760
and explains what all
these sites are all about.

00:47:41.760 --> 00:47:44.760
And he mentions, for instance,
in a somewhat obscure text,

00:47:44.760 --> 00:47:47.133
he's walking in this
very region and he says,

00:47:48.187 --> 00:47:50.820
"By the way, the most important sanctuary

00:47:50.820 --> 00:47:53.187
for the Egyptian goddess Isis is here."

00:47:54.240 --> 00:47:57.990
Me being me, you could
triangulate exactly where it is.

00:47:57.990 --> 00:48:01.710
So I was here a couple years
ago when I started working

00:48:01.710 --> 00:48:04.890
and I said, "What is this little
pin I put in Google maps?"

00:48:04.890 --> 00:48:08.370
I looked at it, I realized I
put it in over 10 years ago

00:48:08.370 --> 00:48:09.397
based upon this text saying,

00:48:09.397 --> 00:48:11.580
"If you've ever near
this area, look at this."

00:48:11.580 --> 00:48:13.470
And what I realized is that I'm here now.

00:48:13.470 --> 00:48:15.450
So I looked, and this
site almost certainly

00:48:15.450 --> 00:48:20.280
is this temple complex of Isis,

00:48:20.280 --> 00:48:22.320
probably at at least in early stage,

00:48:22.320 --> 00:48:24.000
might be a temple to Artemis.

00:48:24.000 --> 00:48:26.670
So like the bookend to
the sanctuary of Apollo.

00:48:26.670 --> 00:48:29.640
And just on the other side of this valley,

00:48:29.640 --> 00:48:32.220
if you guys know legends
just underneath here,

00:48:32.220 --> 00:48:34.290
that's where Oedipus killed
his biological father.

00:48:34.290 --> 00:48:35.940
I mean you can't again make this stuff up.

00:48:35.940 --> 00:48:38.520
It's just steeped in history.

00:48:38.520 --> 00:48:42.030
And what's interesting is
the references to this site,

00:48:42.030 --> 00:48:44.343
Pausanias goes on and on about it.

00:48:45.180 --> 00:48:47.670
It's a mystery, so it can't
go into too many details,

00:48:47.670 --> 00:48:50.550
but it's clear people
came here to get healing.

00:48:50.550 --> 00:48:52.830
Roman emperors came here to get healed,

00:48:52.830 --> 00:48:53.850
never been excavated.

00:48:53.850 --> 00:48:55.650
So again, a very interesting site for us

00:48:55.650 --> 00:48:58.290
to do our type of research
to try to figure out

00:48:58.290 --> 00:49:02.190
what kind of medical approaches
might they have been using

00:49:02.190 --> 00:49:03.573
2000 years ago.

00:49:04.890 --> 00:49:08.910
So just in the time we have, I
think I have a couple minutes

00:49:08.910 --> 00:49:10.633
so you can see how breathtaking it is.

00:49:10.633 --> 00:49:12.780
So this is right, never excavated.

00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:16.050
And the reason why the grass
is, oh, there's actually audio.

00:49:16.050 --> 00:49:17.400
One of my students made it.

00:49:18.420 --> 00:49:21.480
There's so much marble here
that the soil is so poor.

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:23.813
That's why there's probably
grass growing there.

00:49:26.160 --> 00:49:28.927
That's Parnassus, Delphi
is just down the road here.

00:49:28.927 --> 00:49:31.760
(soothing music)

00:49:37.830 --> 00:49:40.950
My previous EVST 40 student
in the first year seminar

00:49:40.950 --> 00:49:43.980
at the Peabody Museum,
he got a UAV license

00:49:43.980 --> 00:49:45.510
and he's the one that did this.

00:49:45.510 --> 00:49:49.233
So wonderful students,
a beautiful landscape.

00:49:51.870 --> 00:49:54.420
So this is gonna be one of our goals for,

00:49:54.420 --> 00:49:56.130
oh by the way, we're flying it from here.

00:49:56.130 --> 00:49:57.060
Thank goodness for the Germans

00:49:57.060 --> 00:49:58.740
building these power supplies.

00:49:58.740 --> 00:50:00.450
They make roads where we could actually do

00:50:00.450 --> 00:50:01.887
this kind of surveying work.

00:50:01.887 --> 00:50:05.190
And there's the site, almost
certainly the original site,

00:50:05.190 --> 00:50:07.440
the upper Acropolis with all the temples,

00:50:07.440 --> 00:50:09.150
and beneath it there was
probably a whole complex

00:50:09.150 --> 00:50:11.190
of medical treatments,

00:50:11.190 --> 00:50:13.350
but nobody traditionally in archeology

00:50:13.350 --> 00:50:17.340
is interested in excavating
a medical complex, right?

00:50:17.340 --> 00:50:18.843
So I sure am.

00:50:21.390 --> 00:50:23.130
So that's kind of where
the future is leading.

00:50:23.130 --> 00:50:25.020
And now the question is what
are the future discoveries

00:50:25.020 --> 00:50:28.209
both here at Yale but also overseas.

00:50:28.209 --> 00:50:29.495
Thank you for your time.

00:50:29.495 --> 00:50:32.495
(audience clapping)

00:50:40.188 --> 00:50:43.680
- Thank you so much for this
utterly fascinating talk.

00:50:43.680 --> 00:50:46.200
I think we are open for Q and A.

00:50:46.200 --> 00:50:47.070
Would you be open-
- Off course, yeah.

00:50:47.070 --> 00:50:48.365
- for questions from
- I'd happy to.

00:50:48.365 --> 00:50:49.770
- The audience, yeah?
- take any questions.

00:50:49.770 --> 00:50:50.670
- Yeah.
- I know it's a lot.

00:50:50.670 --> 00:50:52.650
There's a lot of information, so.

00:50:52.650 --> 00:50:55.020
- [Attendee 1] Thank you
so much for a great talk.

00:50:55.020 --> 00:50:58.110
I'm a forest ecologist, so
I work in the modern day,

00:50:58.110 --> 00:51:02.320
but I'm very curious on when
you're looking at plant species

00:51:03.361 --> 00:51:04.890
that were present in those areas

00:51:04.890 --> 00:51:08.280
and looking at medicinal
uses, what evidences you found

00:51:08.280 --> 00:51:10.290
for trade routes or changes in climate

00:51:10.290 --> 00:51:13.710
and things like that that
have affected both, you know,

00:51:13.710 --> 00:51:15.210
locally available plants,

00:51:15.210 --> 00:51:17.700
but also plants that
were coming from abroad?

00:51:17.700 --> 00:51:18.850
- That's a great point.

00:51:20.430 --> 00:51:23.910
What's interesting about
plants such as Black hellebore

00:51:23.910 --> 00:51:26.220
is that at least the ancient
authors from this time period,

00:51:26.220 --> 00:51:27.480
and again, you gotta always vet them

00:51:27.480 --> 00:51:29.250
and see how much they actually knew.

00:51:29.250 --> 00:51:33.000
They claim what made this
area of Antikyra special

00:51:33.000 --> 00:51:35.250
was these plants, right?

00:51:35.250 --> 00:51:36.630
And they only grew here.

00:51:36.630 --> 00:51:38.190
I mean, what does it mean only grew?

00:51:38.190 --> 00:51:41.280
Is it the specific type
of class of these plants

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:42.870
that they imagined, because
we know Black hellebore

00:51:42.870 --> 00:51:44.460
grew elsewhere as well, right?

00:51:44.460 --> 00:51:49.460
So that once again is why
after a 10, 20 year journey,

00:51:52.650 --> 00:51:55.830
when the opportunity
come to Yale, you know,

00:51:55.830 --> 00:51:59.010
arrived at, you know, just
fortuity came up to me,

00:51:59.010 --> 00:52:01.230
I was at MIT at the time, which was great.

00:52:01.230 --> 00:52:04.710
But obviously in terms of
the humanistic opportunities

00:52:04.710 --> 00:52:06.240
there, they're much more limited.

00:52:06.240 --> 00:52:07.920
All the humanities and
social sciences are basically

00:52:07.920 --> 00:52:10.080
in one building, and on the
other side of the track.

00:52:10.080 --> 00:52:12.360
So it made it difficult for
me to collaborate with them.

00:52:12.360 --> 00:52:14.410
But these are the exact questions

00:52:15.330 --> 00:52:16.170
that we're trying to get to.

00:52:16.170 --> 00:52:19.336
So that's why I don't just
invite archeology students

00:52:19.336 --> 00:52:21.413
or Classists or NELC students to work.

00:52:21.413 --> 00:52:23.880
But the fact that the
School of Environment

00:52:23.880 --> 00:52:26.280
is so closely linked to the museum,

00:52:26.280 --> 00:52:29.880
the director, Dave Skelly
being a part of the school,

00:52:29.880 --> 00:52:31.530
that was highly attractive to me,

00:52:31.530 --> 00:52:34.650
because looking at people
doing green chemistry

00:52:34.650 --> 00:52:37.620
or the, like, the work that you are doing.

00:52:37.620 --> 00:52:41.400
My original GIS mentor, Dana
Tomlin in Philadelphia at Penn,

00:52:41.400 --> 00:52:43.320
he's now here at Yale.

00:52:43.320 --> 00:52:45.540
And he was inspirational, is inspirational

00:52:45.540 --> 00:52:49.653
because in the seventies he
and a whole bunch of his peers,

00:52:50.670 --> 00:52:52.590
remarkably, can you imagine the seventies,

00:52:52.590 --> 00:52:55.500
they helped basically invent GIS, right?

00:52:55.500 --> 00:52:56.760
I mean they ended up, he's the only one

00:52:56.760 --> 00:52:58.020
that stayed in higher education,

00:52:58.020 --> 00:53:02.010
but the rest went on to
establish Esri, IDRISI

00:53:02.010 --> 00:53:03.840
and all kinds of other places.

00:53:03.840 --> 00:53:07.020
So I knew that it was possible at Yale,

00:53:07.020 --> 00:53:09.720
and that's exactly why
I'm here for that reason,

00:53:09.720 --> 00:53:13.950
to start delving into these
very questions of ecology.

00:53:13.950 --> 00:53:16.530
I didn't get into it, but this site itself

00:53:16.530 --> 00:53:19.440
is actually the sister
site to the coastal site.

00:53:19.440 --> 00:53:22.140
And what's neat is we're
starting to take cores

00:53:22.140 --> 00:53:27.140
and things like that, because
there was a lake nearby.

00:53:27.330 --> 00:53:29.790
So just thinking about
this fourth dimensionally

00:53:29.790 --> 00:53:31.740
in terms of time and
everything else like that,

00:53:31.740 --> 00:53:35.130
nearby is a very famous
basin, the Kopais Basin,

00:53:35.130 --> 00:53:39.570
where in this time period
they drain those swampy,

00:53:39.570 --> 00:53:42.000
they drained the entire
land and it's clear

00:53:42.000 --> 00:53:44.070
at the collapse of the Bronze age,

00:53:44.070 --> 00:53:47.370
probably a neighboring town
stuffed their drain hole.

00:53:47.370 --> 00:53:49.500
And it got swampy again.

00:53:49.500 --> 00:53:51.390
And to show you of that accomplishment

00:53:51.390 --> 00:53:54.063
that was done in let's
say the 14th century BCE,

00:53:55.350 --> 00:53:56.730
the Romans, everybody else tried,

00:53:56.730 --> 00:53:57.930
just like with the canal at Corinth.

00:53:57.930 --> 00:54:01.350
They tried repeated
attempts to complete this

00:54:01.350 --> 00:54:03.720
very ambitious engineering work.

00:54:03.720 --> 00:54:06.090
It wasn't until first of French helped.

00:54:06.090 --> 00:54:09.420
So it wasn't drained again
until the 19th century,

00:54:09.420 --> 00:54:10.260
you know, in the modern day.

00:54:10.260 --> 00:54:13.530
So there's all these questions,
we're collecting data,

00:54:13.530 --> 00:54:16.290
we've taken cores in the sea

00:54:16.290 --> 00:54:20.340
to look at the pollen record
and other things like that.

00:54:20.340 --> 00:54:23.790
But reason why have I love doing this

00:54:23.790 --> 00:54:25.410
with an audience like this is

00:54:25.410 --> 00:54:28.709
because then we start
getting ideas and it's,

00:54:28.709 --> 00:54:30.060
like I said, it's about collaboration.

00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:33.150
So we eventually want, we are doing now,

00:54:33.150 --> 00:54:35.070
we're inviting all kinds of scholars.

00:54:35.070 --> 00:54:37.620
There's opportunities
as you can imagine for,

00:54:37.620 --> 00:54:39.480
as one administrator put it,

00:54:39.480 --> 00:54:42.120
there might be somebody from any division,

00:54:42.120 --> 00:54:43.770
department in the university
that could go there

00:54:43.770 --> 00:54:45.150
and find something to do.

00:54:45.150 --> 00:54:48.900
Paleontology, there's fossils
just coming out everywhere.

00:54:48.900 --> 00:54:51.180
So it's a remarkable opportunity

00:54:51.180 --> 00:54:52.950
and definitely ecology is a major part

00:54:52.950 --> 00:54:55.113
of what we're trying to look at.

00:55:09.660 --> 00:55:12.060
- [Attendee 2] Yes, thank
you. Fascinating talk.

00:55:14.294 --> 00:55:18.730
I wonder if DNA is preserved,
I presume it is preserved,

00:55:22.740 --> 00:55:27.417
and if DNA analysis would inform your work

00:55:27.417 --> 00:55:29.190
and your findings.

00:55:29.190 --> 00:55:31.470
- This again, this is
why I love being here,

00:55:31.470 --> 00:55:34.083
because as you might know with,

00:55:35.070 --> 00:55:39.600
there was a major study done
with the Max Planck Institute,

00:55:39.600 --> 00:55:43.680
the Harvard study of the human past.

00:55:43.680 --> 00:55:46.260
David Reich over in the med school.

00:55:46.260 --> 00:55:48.390
They've done some ancient DNA studies,

00:55:48.390 --> 00:55:51.540
but typically, of course,
with human remains.

00:55:51.540 --> 00:55:54.690
And one of the things I
knew, from what I heard,

00:55:54.690 --> 00:55:56.730
like for instance when
ESC was founded here,

00:55:56.730 --> 00:55:58.860
they had some kind of pioneering DNA lab,

00:55:58.860 --> 00:56:01.230
which is now I think
down in the med school.

00:56:01.230 --> 00:56:04.890
But certainly when I
started taking soil samples

00:56:04.890 --> 00:56:06.840
and other things like that, started to see

00:56:06.840 --> 00:56:08.700
where people are with
this type of research.

00:56:08.700 --> 00:56:11.970
There are credible besides
just, you know, pollen cores,

00:56:11.970 --> 00:56:14.220
you know, looking for
pollen and palaeofaeces

00:56:14.220 --> 00:56:15.660
and so on and so forth.

00:56:15.660 --> 00:56:18.360
I think certainly that's
a very promising angle.

00:56:18.360 --> 00:56:21.240
And of course one of the
reasons why I'm in Greece

00:56:21.240 --> 00:56:25.323
is that they are collaborative in ways,

00:56:27.000 --> 00:56:29.430
archeology is so important to them.

00:56:29.430 --> 00:56:31.200
It's both a pro and a con, right?

00:56:31.200 --> 00:56:33.735
Because they are so powerful.

00:56:33.735 --> 00:56:38.220
It's arguably the most important
branch of their government

00:56:38.220 --> 00:56:41.040
because of the 40% of their
income comes from tourism,

00:56:41.040 --> 00:56:42.120
as you might know.

00:56:42.120 --> 00:56:47.120
So it could create a
certain kind of bureaucracy,

00:56:47.400 --> 00:56:50.430
but at the same time
people don't ask questions

00:56:50.430 --> 00:56:52.710
when it comes to the science part.

00:56:52.710 --> 00:56:54.690
They absolutely want the
best science to be done.

00:56:54.690 --> 00:56:58.057
And an example of that
is in just neighbor,

00:56:58.057 --> 00:56:59.820
it's not necessarily good or bad,

00:56:59.820 --> 00:57:03.090
but it's the system over in Italy,

00:57:03.090 --> 00:57:05.640
you can't really sample or study.

00:57:05.640 --> 00:57:08.430
Even if you want a UAV
photogrammetry scan,

00:57:08.430 --> 00:57:11.610
an invert paleontology sample,
they won't from I heard.

00:57:11.610 --> 00:57:15.180
The Italian government system
is such that you can't do it.

00:57:15.180 --> 00:57:19.980
I proposed that in mark
Parnassus, it's like an up zone.

00:57:19.980 --> 00:57:22.297
And they actually said to me,

00:57:22.297 --> 00:57:23.670
"Why do you want us just scan it?

00:57:23.670 --> 00:57:25.590
Why don't you just, we'll chop
it and we'll give it to you

00:57:25.590 --> 00:57:26.820
and you can take it to New Haven."

00:57:26.820 --> 00:57:29.460
We're like, "Okay, we
probably don't wanna do that."

00:57:29.460 --> 00:57:31.110
But that shows you how collaborative

00:57:31.110 --> 00:57:33.480
and cooperative they are.

00:57:33.480 --> 00:57:34.860
I love working in Egypt.

00:57:34.860 --> 00:57:38.970
I love my colleagues there,
but they have a strict rule,

00:57:38.970 --> 00:57:41.100
even if it's a toy under 250 grams,

00:57:41.100 --> 00:57:42.480
which is the definition of, I think,

00:57:42.480 --> 00:57:45.450
in the field in this UAV technology

00:57:45.450 --> 00:57:47.580
with how the world recognizes it.

00:57:47.580 --> 00:57:51.000
You can't fly single UAV in Egypt.

00:57:51.000 --> 00:57:55.350
And our collaborators,
Nadine Moeller at NELC,

00:57:55.350 --> 00:57:58.980
and Greg, her co-director there,

00:57:58.980 --> 00:58:02.340
they're having a heck of a
time, like, how do you actually

00:58:02.340 --> 00:58:05.400
basically not have this major
technology in archeology?

00:58:05.400 --> 00:58:06.750
I mean, we're doing thermal imaging,

00:58:06.750 --> 00:58:08.640
we're doing multispectral analysis.

00:58:08.640 --> 00:58:12.180
Going back to the ecology
of it, it's absolutely,

00:58:12.180 --> 00:58:13.680
as I pointed out, the multispectral work

00:58:13.680 --> 00:58:15.150
that we're testing out in Horse Island

00:58:15.150 --> 00:58:17.460
and we're starting to use in Greece.

00:58:17.460 --> 00:58:19.560
We're happily sharing this
technology, and information,

00:58:19.560 --> 00:58:24.560
and data with all the faculty
and collaborators in Greece.

00:58:25.020 --> 00:58:27.540
And almost exclusively,
this is very interesting.

00:58:27.540 --> 00:58:29.370
It's not the archeologists
that want the data,

00:58:29.370 --> 00:58:31.350
it's all the scientists that want it.

00:58:31.350 --> 00:58:34.947
So that's neat to me
that, I'm very flattered,

00:58:34.947 --> 00:58:37.303
and humble that the archeologists that,

00:58:37.303 --> 00:58:39.180
I mean, we work with archeologists,

00:58:39.180 --> 00:58:41.580
but the scientists are
perhaps the most excited

00:58:41.580 --> 00:58:44.670
over in Greece about this, which is neat.

00:58:44.670 --> 00:58:47.340
But DNA, yeah, we want to get there too,

00:58:47.340 --> 00:58:48.570
because you're absolutely right.

00:58:48.570 --> 00:58:50.520
I know what you're thinking
about with the plants

00:58:50.520 --> 00:58:53.460
and the Vienna Dioscorides,
but you've read my mind

00:58:53.460 --> 00:58:55.793
that that's certainly an
angle we wanna pursue.

00:59:10.830 --> 00:59:12.300
- [Attendee 3] So some of the compounds

00:59:12.300 --> 00:59:14.940
that modern pharmacologists study.

00:59:14.940 --> 00:59:16.920
- Sure.
- Came from

00:59:16.920 --> 00:59:20.103
ethno pharmacologists or people who,

00:59:21.060 --> 00:59:23.520
even amateurs who just go around the world

00:59:23.520 --> 00:59:26.040
trying to sample drugs
that people are taking now.

00:59:26.040 --> 00:59:27.660
- That's right.

00:59:27.660 --> 00:59:30.360
- [Attendee 3] And I'm just wondering,

00:59:30.360 --> 00:59:34.380
in any of the locations
where there's archeologically

00:59:34.380 --> 00:59:39.380
interesting chemical use, are
there any remnants of that

00:59:40.500 --> 00:59:43.383
among the local populations in terms of,

00:59:45.510 --> 00:59:48.450
you know, plants that
people use for various,

00:59:48.450 --> 00:59:51.690
usually it's something that
has an effect on the brain.

00:59:51.690 --> 00:59:53.986
Those are the things that
people tend to pick up.

00:59:53.986 --> 00:59:55.770
- That's the thing, it's like,

00:59:55.770 --> 00:59:58.863
one of the things I think
we're doing is that just like,

01:00:00.210 --> 01:00:02.790
when you look at the Vienna Dioscorides,

01:00:02.790 --> 01:00:05.193
we say book five is about food,

01:00:06.150 --> 01:00:09.870
book one, one part of it
is about psychedelics.

01:00:09.870 --> 01:00:11.190
These are modern terms.

01:00:11.190 --> 01:00:14.160
Like it's clear that they
kind of loosely put them

01:00:14.160 --> 01:00:17.010
into these books, but they
didn't mean to categorize it

01:00:17.010 --> 01:00:18.300
that distinctly, right?

01:00:18.300 --> 01:00:19.770
These are just organics you consume

01:00:19.770 --> 01:00:21.540
and it somehow affects your body.

01:00:21.540 --> 01:00:23.010
I'm not saying that we
should get rid of categories

01:00:23.010 --> 01:00:25.620
in the modern day, but I'm just proposing,

01:00:25.620 --> 01:00:27.990
maybe we've gotta kind of think
outside the box a little bit

01:00:27.990 --> 01:00:30.540
and think about how all of these things,

01:00:30.540 --> 01:00:33.750
like how do our modern
systems and sensibilities

01:00:33.750 --> 01:00:35.820
affect the way that we interpret it,

01:00:35.820 --> 01:00:37.533
and look at these things.

01:00:38.700 --> 01:00:39.660
What I'm trying to get back to,

01:00:39.660 --> 01:00:41.190
an example of what you just mentioned

01:00:41.190 --> 01:00:45.150
is that in south of our site,
in the Peloponnese there,

01:00:45.150 --> 01:00:46.560
in Arcadia, up in the hills,

01:00:46.560 --> 01:00:49.320
that's often where the interesting
information comes from.

01:00:49.320 --> 01:00:51.420
I still remember this, and
I'm gonna try to go back to it

01:00:51.420 --> 01:00:53.400
because I was just passing through there,

01:00:53.400 --> 01:00:55.890
and I was kind of doing, you
know, this kind of research.

01:00:55.890 --> 01:00:57.990
I didn't have a program,
again, a square peg,

01:00:57.990 --> 01:01:00.593
trying to figure out which
department I was gonna be in.

01:01:01.500 --> 01:01:03.330
And we met a local and said, "Oh,

01:01:03.330 --> 01:01:05.880
my family for centuries was doing

01:01:05.880 --> 01:01:08.820
what you would call maybe pharmacology."

01:01:08.820 --> 01:01:10.440
So she said, "Do you wanna see it?"

01:01:10.440 --> 01:01:12.000
So I went to their shop and you know,

01:01:12.000 --> 01:01:14.730
there's newspaper on the frosty glass,

01:01:14.730 --> 01:01:17.490
it's probably hadn't been
used for a generation.

01:01:17.490 --> 01:01:20.460
She lets me in, it took my
breath away because it has,

01:01:20.460 --> 01:01:25.320
it's probably maybe something
more along the lines

01:01:25.320 --> 01:01:27.203
of what we'll call like an apothecary.

01:01:28.530 --> 01:01:32.040
So the beautiful wooden
shelve and everything else,

01:01:32.040 --> 01:01:35.730
like the beautiful glass jars.

01:01:35.730 --> 01:01:38.610
And now it kind of, I gotta get back there

01:01:38.610 --> 01:01:40.530
and I'm not sure, I mean,
I'm not holding out hope,

01:01:40.530 --> 01:01:42.210
it's anything still there,

01:01:42.210 --> 01:01:45.360
but all the glass jars were
labeled in the beautiful,

01:01:45.360 --> 01:01:47.815
more modern Greek script.

01:01:47.815 --> 01:01:50.970
And then they specifically
labeled not just

01:01:50.970 --> 01:01:53.430
how they viewed it as a substance,

01:01:53.430 --> 01:01:56.760
but also there was their
details about what plants,

01:01:56.760 --> 01:01:58.830
where they collected it from.

01:01:58.830 --> 01:02:01.450
And I'll give you a, I
don't usually like to

01:02:03.450 --> 01:02:07.350
kind of be a downer, but
let me just share you

01:02:07.350 --> 01:02:09.330
where is that, hope I can find that.

01:02:09.330 --> 01:02:11.940
So I knew, there is a sense of urgency.

01:02:11.940 --> 01:02:16.940
Like I don't like to necessarily
push people to scare them,

01:02:18.900 --> 01:02:20.250
but I'll give you an example.

01:02:20.250 --> 01:02:23.283
So these three individuals,

01:02:24.600 --> 01:02:27.240
they lived up in that mountain village,

01:02:27.240 --> 01:02:30.180
and we did an interview with them.

01:02:30.180 --> 01:02:31.897
And they're telling us
all this information,

01:02:31.897 --> 01:02:32.790
"Oh, you're looking for this."

01:02:32.790 --> 01:02:34.297
They loved us, they're like,

01:02:34.297 --> 01:02:36.840
"Nobody in the younger
generations really at all

01:02:36.840 --> 01:02:38.820
interested in this kind of research."

01:02:38.820 --> 01:02:41.100
So we videotaped it with their consent,

01:02:41.100 --> 01:02:42.930
we wrote it all down,
this is just a start.

01:02:42.930 --> 01:02:45.270
We're gonna get all
this great information.

01:02:45.270 --> 01:02:48.483
2018, that winter, all
three of them passed away.

01:02:49.980 --> 01:02:51.120
That's what's going on.

01:02:51.120 --> 01:02:52.860
And we thought all was kind of lost.

01:02:52.860 --> 01:02:55.050
And what ended up happening was,

01:02:55.050 --> 01:02:58.830
I showed you that earlier
picture with the gentleman.

01:02:58.830 --> 01:03:03.690
We had the sample of Black hellebore.

01:03:03.690 --> 01:03:05.730
And by some miracle we found him,

01:03:05.730 --> 01:03:10.350
he's actually the grandson of
the individual in the middle.

01:03:10.350 --> 01:03:11.910
And he said, he's in the sixties,

01:03:11.910 --> 01:03:15.990
and he said, "Look, I'm the
last, at least in this area.

01:03:15.990 --> 01:03:19.770
I think I'm gonna be alive
for a number more of winters,

01:03:19.770 --> 01:03:21.720
but if you're gonna do this

01:03:21.720 --> 01:03:23.880
and get information from
me, it's the time now.

01:03:23.880 --> 01:03:25.590
I'm not sure if I'm gonna live."

01:03:25.590 --> 01:03:27.570
You know, he's like 68 or
something like that, right?

01:03:27.570 --> 01:03:29.760
And he said there's a sense of urgency.

01:03:29.760 --> 01:03:32.850
So that's why I realized I need this team

01:03:32.850 --> 01:03:33.870
of research assistants.

01:03:33.870 --> 01:03:35.880
We're gonna go there this
summer, for instance,

01:03:35.880 --> 01:03:38.130
and we're gonna go to every single valley

01:03:38.130 --> 01:03:41.760
he points out and document
in every fashion if possible.

01:03:41.760 --> 01:03:45.060
And you can imagine we have
support at the highest levels

01:03:45.060 --> 01:03:49.200
from the Greek ministries,
because they understandably know

01:03:49.200 --> 01:03:52.050
that we're passionate about this,

01:03:52.050 --> 01:03:55.080
but also working with the locals.

01:03:55.080 --> 01:03:58.290
They, again, this is not something

01:03:58.290 --> 01:03:59.520
that you gotta be worried about,

01:03:59.520 --> 01:04:00.570
but there's a certain relationship

01:04:00.570 --> 01:04:03.540
between foreign institutions
of archeology, you know,

01:04:03.540 --> 01:04:04.950
foreign institutions
like the American school

01:04:04.950 --> 01:04:07.080
has been working there
since the 19th century.

01:04:07.080 --> 01:04:07.980
There's a certain, let's just say

01:04:07.980 --> 01:04:10.110
there's a certain
relationship that's developed

01:04:10.110 --> 01:04:13.080
in the 150 years, and that's
kind of the status quo.

01:04:13.080 --> 01:04:16.560
So what, when we come in and
we work in the way we do,

01:04:16.560 --> 01:04:19.680
as I pointed out, the
locals are absolutely,

01:04:19.680 --> 01:04:22.410
one, flabbergasted, but
obviously they're very supportive

01:04:22.410 --> 01:04:26.520
of what we do because we work
hand in hand with the locals.

01:04:26.520 --> 01:04:28.500
I don't think I'm being that
quote unquote disruptive.

01:04:28.500 --> 01:04:31.380
I'm just doing it the way
I think it should be done.

01:04:31.380 --> 01:04:32.213
So, yep.

01:04:40.320 --> 01:04:41.730
That's a great question.

01:04:41.730 --> 01:04:43.813
We're trying. (laughs)

01:04:49.501 --> 01:04:51.360
- [Attendee 4] I just wonder,
I was very curious because,

01:04:51.360 --> 01:04:53.370
there are these traditions,

01:04:53.370 --> 01:04:56.400
current practice traditions like Ayurveda,

01:04:56.400 --> 01:05:01.200
which is really based on
plant science and you know,

01:05:01.200 --> 01:05:06.026
there are treatises on which
similarly I wonder, right?

01:05:06.026 --> 01:05:07.680
I mean, I have heard in India

01:05:07.680 --> 01:05:11.760
there is a scheme of medicine
called Unani medicine,

01:05:11.760 --> 01:05:13.470
which is Greek.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:05:13.470 --> 01:05:16.320
- [Attendee 4] Yeah, so I was
curious whether these modern

01:05:16.320 --> 01:05:20.250
alternative practices
actually also contain

01:05:20.250 --> 01:05:23.400
some of the information
that you're looking for,

01:05:23.400 --> 01:05:26.070
or do you think these are
actually too contaminated

01:05:26.070 --> 01:05:27.843
by now over time?

01:05:28.890 --> 01:05:32.100
- The way I explained
it is I view all data

01:05:32.100 --> 01:05:35.580
as valuable data, and
you're absolutely correct.

01:05:35.580 --> 01:05:38.167
Like, people ask me,

01:05:38.167 --> 01:05:42.176
"Why did you major in biophysics
and Classics and NELC?"

01:05:42.176 --> 01:05:45.600
And this probably wouldn't
surprise many of you,

01:05:45.600 --> 01:05:46.980
bluntly, my parents would've killed me

01:05:46.980 --> 01:05:51.060
if I majored in Classics,
as I was pre-med.

01:05:51.060 --> 01:05:53.340
So it was kind of a,
it was detente, right?

01:05:53.340 --> 01:05:58.340
So it thrilled me when my
student Ria, who you met,

01:05:59.820 --> 01:06:03.963
doing ethnobotany, she's from
South Asia and she was just,

01:06:04.860 --> 01:06:06.810
you know, FaceTiming with her parents,

01:06:06.810 --> 01:06:10.410
and they were like, "Oh, you're
working in the lab you got.

01:06:10.410 --> 01:06:12.143
Is your professor there,
or your director?"

01:06:12.143 --> 01:06:13.140
And she said, "Yeah."

01:06:13.140 --> 01:06:16.920
So I talked to them and
they were so thankful,

01:06:16.920 --> 01:06:19.830
because I mean, just to
be again very honest,

01:06:19.830 --> 01:06:21.660
my parents would be like, "Ethnobotany?"

01:06:21.660 --> 01:06:23.100
Like, "What are you doing here?"

01:06:23.100 --> 01:06:26.640
But her parents were
like so thankful, saying,

01:06:26.640 --> 01:06:28.230
and they're explaining to
me all these traditions.

01:06:28.230 --> 01:06:29.457
And I said, "We're absolutely
gonna pursue that."

01:06:29.457 --> 01:06:31.380
And that's why we need people

01:06:31.380 --> 01:06:33.510
from all over the world
who are international,

01:06:33.510 --> 01:06:35.760
because we're gonna pursue those things.

01:06:35.760 --> 01:06:37.470
It's a wealth of information because-

01:06:37.470 --> 01:06:40.590
- [Attendee 4] Are they surviving
such traditions in Greece?

01:06:40.590 --> 01:06:41.994
I mean, is there-

01:06:41.994 --> 01:06:44.730
- They're often-
- (talking indistinctly)

01:06:44.730 --> 01:06:46.860
- There often is, but it's missed, right?

01:06:46.860 --> 01:06:49.920
Like, I mean if they're not,
if people aren't catching,

01:06:49.920 --> 01:06:51.540
and I'm not saying that
they can do everything,

01:06:51.540 --> 01:06:53.040
but it's just where
people are focusing now

01:06:53.040 --> 01:06:54.930
and these traditions
have been established.

01:06:54.930 --> 01:06:56.790
Like the classics, no one reads

01:06:56.790 --> 01:06:58.410
really the Vienna Dioscorides, you know,

01:06:58.410 --> 01:07:00.480
De materia medica, so on and so forth.

01:07:00.480 --> 01:07:03.610
Another quick example,
which is remarkable, is

01:07:04.710 --> 01:07:09.710
the last generation who spoke
the native Mayan language,

01:07:11.940 --> 01:07:14.310
they also of course
knew the Mayan language,

01:07:14.310 --> 01:07:18.660
but they also knew, they
learned Spanish and Latin.

01:07:18.660 --> 01:07:21.300
So this is the first
interface between the worlds,

01:07:21.300 --> 01:07:23.010
and there's this wonderful beautiful book.

01:07:23.010 --> 01:07:25.230
It surprised me even more in a sense

01:07:25.230 --> 01:07:26.063
than the Vienna Dioscorides,

01:07:26.063 --> 01:07:28.500
because I had no idea this book existed.

01:07:28.500 --> 01:07:31.680
It was lost for centuries, right?

01:07:31.680 --> 01:07:34.643
It was written the, it's similar
to the Vienna Dioscorides,

01:07:34.643 --> 01:07:37.380
it talk about all the
plants in the Americas

01:07:37.380 --> 01:07:38.700
and so on and so forth.

01:07:38.700 --> 01:07:43.240
Then it was sent, eventually
landed in the Vatican Library

01:07:44.190 --> 01:07:48.180
where it was forgotten for
centuries until the 1930s.

01:07:48.180 --> 01:07:51.090
The Pope gifted it to Mexico,

01:07:51.090 --> 01:07:52.890
and there was one translation done then,

01:07:52.890 --> 01:07:54.150
and then it was forgotten again.

01:07:54.150 --> 01:07:57.330
So that's a parallel example

01:07:57.330 --> 01:07:59.070
where we're looking at these traditions,

01:07:59.070 --> 01:08:00.870
and then we're talking to the locals.

01:08:00.870 --> 01:08:03.903
And what's also remarkable is that,

01:08:05.070 --> 01:08:08.580
for our herbarium is very
strong in the Americas,

01:08:08.580 --> 01:08:10.260
and all of a sudden it's like a bridge.

01:08:10.260 --> 01:08:11.700
But we're gonna collect more specimens

01:08:11.700 --> 01:08:14.520
with the collaboration to
Greeks to add to our herbarium.

01:08:14.520 --> 01:08:16.110
But it's another base of operations

01:08:16.110 --> 01:08:17.670
'cause there's lots of parallels.

01:08:17.670 --> 01:08:21.330
So it sounds ambitious and we
are very sensitive to that.

01:08:21.330 --> 01:08:23.400
We wanna make sure what
we do is achievable

01:08:23.400 --> 01:08:25.050
as much as possible, but again,

01:08:25.050 --> 01:08:26.317
this is why we love talking to different,

01:08:26.317 --> 01:08:27.960
you know, different units,

01:08:27.960 --> 01:08:30.420
whether it's people that
focus on South Asia,

01:08:30.420 --> 01:08:32.733
or Central America, so on and so forth.

01:08:34.080 --> 01:08:35.480
That's a great point though.

01:08:39.630 --> 01:08:42.720
- [Attendee 5] I recall
that there was a substantial

01:08:42.720 --> 01:08:46.620
medical establishment
in what is now called

01:08:46.620 --> 01:08:48.930
Pergamon in Turkey.

01:08:48.930 --> 01:08:53.220
And I wondered whether that
being substantially later

01:08:53.220 --> 01:08:57.000
than bronze age place,

01:08:57.000 --> 01:09:01.200
but I wondered whether there
was any information there,

01:09:01.200 --> 01:09:05.400
and if there is more
interest in your approach,

01:09:05.400 --> 01:09:10.400
whether the Turkish government
is in any way helpful.

01:09:10.530 --> 01:09:13.020
- That's a great point
because, of course Pergamon

01:09:13.020 --> 01:09:14.790
was where Galen operated from,

01:09:14.790 --> 01:09:17.730
who was a very famous physician
whose treatises were used

01:09:17.730 --> 01:09:19.593
until fairly modern days.

01:09:20.670 --> 01:09:23.130
So next to the Archegesion
where we're working

01:09:23.130 --> 01:09:24.990
is Asklepeion of course.

01:09:24.990 --> 01:09:28.260
And that's exactly of course
the context in Pergamon

01:09:28.260 --> 01:09:29.730
that Galen was operating in.

01:09:29.730 --> 01:09:31.800
Again, one of these leading edge

01:09:31.800 --> 01:09:34.290
medical facilities at the time.

01:09:34.290 --> 01:09:36.767
Of course the library was considered

01:09:36.767 --> 01:09:41.767
only second to the library
at Alexandria, of course.

01:09:42.240 --> 01:09:47.220
What we think is clearly Dioscorides

01:09:47.220 --> 01:09:49.500
had access to the library at Alexandria,

01:09:49.500 --> 01:09:51.000
and maybe at Pergamon,

01:09:51.000 --> 01:09:54.003
because he comes from what's
today like Southeast Turkey.

01:09:55.020 --> 01:09:57.480
And it just so happens in September,

01:09:57.480 --> 01:10:02.480
I was at the modern, what's
called in the modern day

01:10:04.350 --> 01:10:08.253
Kültepe-Kanesh, which is
a very famous site there,

01:10:09.510 --> 01:10:12.660
Gojko Barjamovic who is an
Assyriologist at Harvard,

01:10:12.660 --> 01:10:14.100
and married to our curator here,

01:10:14.100 --> 01:10:15.420
was kind enough to take me around

01:10:15.420 --> 01:10:17.760
after this event in Eleusis in Greece.

01:10:17.760 --> 01:10:20.820
Remarkable, and what, again,
broke my heart in a good way

01:10:20.820 --> 01:10:24.900
was we're sitting there at Kültepe

01:10:24.900 --> 01:10:26.707
and the director Fikri says,

01:10:26.707 --> 01:10:28.290
"Oh, you're interested
in this kind of stuff.

01:10:28.290 --> 01:10:30.960
You know, like right over that hill

01:10:30.960 --> 01:10:33.990
is where Dioscorides grew up and lived."

01:10:33.990 --> 01:10:34.947
And he's like, "Do you
wanna go there now?"

01:10:34.947 --> 01:10:37.500
And we're like, look like
we're gonna get to the airport

01:10:37.500 --> 01:10:39.033
and just like, so one day.

01:10:39.930 --> 01:10:41.760
But he said, "Oh, if you want."

01:10:41.760 --> 01:10:43.187
Again, working with locals is so important

01:10:43.187 --> 01:10:46.350
'cause he said that, I know
Turkey has some different rules

01:10:46.350 --> 01:10:48.180
and they're trying to, they
were at our acer meeting

01:10:48.180 --> 01:10:49.650
going around saying,
"We know that the rules

01:10:49.650 --> 01:10:51.270
have been kind of draconian of late."

01:10:51.270 --> 01:10:53.107
The consulate showed up and said,

01:10:53.107 --> 01:10:54.570
"Please, we've loosened these rules,

01:10:54.570 --> 01:10:56.520
come and work in Turkey."

01:10:56.520 --> 01:10:57.720
Coming up to each of us.

01:10:57.720 --> 01:10:59.730
They were reading our abstracts.

01:10:59.730 --> 01:11:00.563
They came to me and said,

01:11:00.563 --> 01:11:02.160
"You're interested in pharmacology."

01:11:02.160 --> 01:11:02.993
Where I'm like, "Yes."

01:11:02.993 --> 01:11:04.620
And they said, "Please come."

01:11:04.620 --> 01:11:05.910
So we're working with them.

01:11:05.910 --> 01:11:09.000
And Fikri said, "Look, next time you come,

01:11:09.000 --> 01:11:10.920
all those plants that
Dioscorides talks about,

01:11:10.920 --> 01:11:12.060
we'll get them for you."

01:11:12.060 --> 01:11:13.020
And I thought, "Oh my gosh."

01:11:13.020 --> 01:11:16.260
I mean within kilometers
of where he's writing

01:11:16.260 --> 01:11:17.790
about these kind of things, right?

01:11:17.790 --> 01:11:20.100
So you can imagine, and then of course,

01:11:20.100 --> 01:11:21.480
Istanbul, Constantinople,

01:11:21.480 --> 01:11:24.300
which is where this
volume was used, right?

01:11:24.300 --> 01:11:27.840
And written, I'm very thankful

01:11:27.840 --> 01:11:30.360
for the different units here,

01:11:30.360 --> 01:11:32.700
because I'm not an expert in

01:11:32.700 --> 01:11:34.167
the Turkish language for instance.

01:11:34.167 --> 01:11:36.540
But I could go to my colleagues and NELC,

01:11:36.540 --> 01:11:39.450
and you know, find somebody
that knows something

01:11:39.450 --> 01:11:44.450
about something and it
kind of really supports,

01:11:46.890 --> 01:11:49.470
and makes me feel good
about making the move

01:11:49.470 --> 01:11:51.670
from Cambridge to New
Haven for that reason.

01:11:54.930 --> 01:11:57.660
- I wanna take this up and
just to thank you once more.

01:11:57.660 --> 01:12:00.510
And I think it's remarkable that a lot

01:12:00.510 --> 01:12:04.350
of big pharma companies
are also in the search

01:12:04.350 --> 01:12:07.830
for new natural compounds that we can get,

01:12:07.830 --> 01:12:10.950
and they're scouring the world
for indigenous knowledge.

01:12:10.950 --> 01:12:12.810
So thank you so much for what you do,

01:12:12.810 --> 01:12:15.120
and it was really exciting
for us to hear about it.

01:12:15.120 --> 01:12:17.367
So let me just remind everyone.

01:12:17.367 --> 01:12:20.367
(audience clapping)

01:12:23.509 --> 01:12:26.342
(soothing music)

